var tafsirData_10 = {
  "1": "“Alif-Lãm-Ra. These are the verses of the Book, rich in wisdom” opens the chapter with the same mystery and majesty that began the previous surah with Alif-Lãm-Mĩm-Ṣãd. The letters, or “Alif-Lãm-Ra,” are among the disconnected letters whose knowledge is with Allah, recited as names, reminding that the Quran explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, whose Word is perfected, is composed of the same letters that challenged “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables” and “They wish to extinguish light with mouths but Allah perfects it.” The description “These are the verses of the Book, rich in wisdom” or “tilka ayatu al-kitabi al-hakeem” names them “hakeem,” wise, full of wisdom, the same wisdom of the All-Wise, All-Knowing who wrote twelve months in the Record, lightened the burden knowing weakness, and is ever gracious to the hard-hour followers. Hakeem here is like the Tablets’ fundamentals and the best of the Tablets, the straight path made clear to the mindful, the insight, guide, and mercy.\n\nThis opening links directly to the previous surah’s closing “Allah is sufficient for me, Lord of the Mighty Throne,” now introducing wisdom’s Book, showing sufficiency is not solitary courage but wisdom’s verses. It also links to the next astonishment: is it astonishing that We sent revelation to a man from among themselves to warn and give good news that they will have honourable status, yet disbelievers said “This man is clearly a magician,” the same magician accusation given to Moses’s staff-snake and the Quran’s fables accusation.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats wisdom as abstract philosophy. The Quran’s wisdom is the practical Hakeem of “Whether you donate small or large, or cross a valley it is written for best reward” and “It is not necessary for all to march, but a party should deepen understanding then warn so perhaps they will beware,” the same wisdom that varied lessons for the thankful and that varied the alms for eight. For the heedless like cattle and the dog panting, Alif-Lãm-Ra is a sign to listen attentively and be silent so mercy is shown, to remember inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness, like angels nearest not too proud to glorify and prostrate.",
  "2": "“Is it astonishing to people that We have sent revelation to a man from among themselves, ˹instructing him,˺ ‘Warn humanity and give good news to the believers that they will have an honourable status with their Lord.’? Yet the disbelievers said, ‘Indeed, this ˹man˺ is clearly a magician!’” questions the same astonishment that made Noah’s and Hud’s peoples call their brothers misguided or fool, and that made the Tabuk rich ask to be left. The phrase “Is it astonishing to people that We have sent revelation to a man from among themselves, instructing him, ‘Warn humanity and give good news to the believers that they will have an honourable status with their Lord.’?” or “a-kana li-nasi ajaban an awhaina ila rajulin minhum an anthiri al-nasa wa bashshiri allatheena amanoo anna lahum qadama sidqin inda Rabbihim” uses “ajaban,” wonder, astonishment, as earlier “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder comes through one of your own so he may warn you” and “Do you find it astonishing a reminder comes through one of your own so you may beware and be shown mercy.” The instruction, warn, or “anthiri,” is the same warner who is only a clear warner, not insane, whose surah revealed stating “Believe and struggle with the Messenger” tests, and whose ear is good for you, whose sufficiency is Allah with followers. The good news, or “bashshiri,” that believers will have “qadama sidqin,” honourable status, foothold of truth, at their Lord, is the same true foothold as the elevated ranks, forgiveness, honourable provision, mercy, pleasure, Gardens forever, splendid homes, greatest triumph, where the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured and the prepared Gardens await.\n\nThe disbelievers’ retort “Indeed, this man is clearly a magician!” or “inna hatha la-sahirun mubeen” repeats the “skilled magician who seeks to drive you from land” from Pharaoh’s chiefs and the “He made it up, produce a surah like it” challenge, the same batil that was devoured when truth prevailed.\n\nThis verse links Alif-Lãm-Ra wisdom to the human messenger, showing wisdom’s Book comes via a man from among themselves so it can be understood, as Moses was told to hold what was given and be grateful, and as the Messenger is concerned by your suffering, anxious, gracious, merciful. A common error deems a human warner too ordinary for such news. The Quran says the honourable foothold with the Lord is precisely why the warner is from among them, so the assent “Yes, we testify” to “Am I not your Lord?” can be renewed, and so the weighing just and the barrier may be told.",
  "3": "“Surely your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne, conducting every affair. None can intercede except by His permission. That is Allah—your Lord, so worship Him ˹alone˺. Will you not then be mindful?” establishes Lordship after the astonishment at the human warner. The previous verse’s magician accusation denied the messenger’s honourable status; this verse grounds that status in the Lord who creates and governs. The phrase “Surely your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne, conducting every affair” or “inna Rabbakum Allahu allathee khalaqa al-samawati wal-arda fi sittati ayyamin thumma istawa ala al-arshi yudabbiru al-amra” repeats the creation in six Days then establishment on the Throne, conducting, or “yudabbiru,” every affair, the same “creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed” whose winds produce heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit, whose twelve-month Record is written, whose angels are supported with help and believers’ hearts united. Conducting every affair includes the fine phases of sun and moon with precisely ordained stages so you may know number of years and calculation, the next verse, and the dream few, the double fewness, the valley appointment, the drowsiness and rain, all affairs.\n\nThe intercession clause “None can intercede except by His permission” or “ma min shafee‘in illa min ba‘di ithnihi” answers the later “They worship besides Allah others who can neither harm nor benefit and say ‘These are our intercessors’” and “Are you informing Allah of something He does not know? Glorified above what they associate” and the earlier “Those to whom We gave the Book know it has been revealed from your Lord, so do not doubt” and the “five for close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers” distribution by Allah and Messenger, showing intercession is not a right claimed like guardian-ship of the Sacred Mosque by those who hinder, but a permission granted to the mindful, as on the Day they will be gathered and associate-gods will say “It was not us you worshipped, Allah is witness we were unaware.”\n\nThe call “That is Allah—your Lord, so worship Him alone. Will you not then be mindful?” or “thalikum Allahu Rabbukum fa-‘budoohu a-fa-la tathakkaroon” is the same “That is Allah your Lord, Creator of all things, worship Him, Maintainer” and “tathakkaroon,” will you be mindful, the same perhaps you will beware, perhaps you will be mindful, that varies lessons for the thankful.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats intercession as automatic for any idol called partner. The verse says none except by His permission, whose permission is for the truth-guiding, justice-establishing community, the hopeful on the Heights told “Enter Paradise, no fear,” and the those who repent and are devoted, praise, fast, bow, prostrate, enjoin, forbid, observe limits, given good news.",
  "4": "“To Him is your return all together. Allah’s promise is ˹always˺ true. Indeed, He originates the creation then resurrects it so that He may justly reward those who believe and do good. But those who disbelieve will have a boiling drink and a painful punishment for their disbelief” follows the conducting of every affair with the ultimate return. The phrase “To Him is your return all together. Allah’s promise is always true” or “ilaihi marji‘ukum jamee‘an wa‘da Allahi haqqan” uses “marji‘,” return, the same gathering where to Him all matters will be returned for judgment, where to Him you will be gathered and Allah stands between person and heart, where U-turn is to the Knower who will inform you, where the Horn will be blown and all authority is His on the Trumpet day. “Wa‘da Allahi haqqan,” Allah’s promise is true, is the true promise binding on Him in Torah, Gospel, Quran, whose Word is perfected, whose purchase is lives and wealth for Paradise ultimate triumph, whose noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, whose prior decree made enjoyment lawful and good.\n\nThe purpose “Indeed, He originates the creation then resurrects it so that He may justly reward those who believe and do good” or “innahu yabda’u al-khalqa thumma yu‘eeduhu li-yajziya allatheena amanoo wa amiloo al-salihati bil-qist” repeats the fertile-infertile logic and the winds’ heavy clouds producing every fruit similarly raising the dead so perhaps you will be mindful. Origin, or “yabda’u,” is like originating from a single soul then made spouse for tranquility, then light burden growing heavy, prayed “If You grant good offspring we will be grateful,” now the resurrection completes gratitude or association. Justly, or “bil-qist,” by equity, mirrors giving full measure and weighing with justice, entering humbly saying “Absolve us,” and alms for eight as obligation.\n\nThe counterpart “But those who disbelieve will have a boiling drink and a painful punishment for their disbelief” or “wa allatheena kafaroo lahum sharabun min hameemin wa athabun aleemun bima kanu yakfuroon” is the same boiling, or “hameem,” scalding water that the opposite of rivers beneath, the same taste that angels beating faces and backs say “Taste burning,” the same taste for the whistling prayer and the disbelievers’ spending to hinder until regret.\n\nA common error sees return as distant. The verse says promise is true, origin proves return, purpose is just reward for belief and good versus boiling for disbelief, inviting the astonishing to move from “This man is magician” to the mindful who see signs in alternation of night and day and all He created, truly signs for the mindful.",
  "5": "“He is the One Who made the sun a radiant source and the moon a reflected light, with precisely ordained phases, so that you may know the number of years and calculation ˹of time˺. Allah did not create all this except for a purpose. He makes the signs clear for people of knowledge” details the conducting of every affair just mentioned, turning heavens into a calendar. The phrase “He is the One Who made the sun a radiant source and the moon a reflected light, with precisely ordained phases” or “huwa allathee ja‘ala al-shamsa diya’an wa al-qamara nooran wa qaddarahu manazila” uses “diya’,” radiant source, and “noor,” reflected light, the same pair that earlier were travelling with precision, or “husban,” and with phases, or “manazil,” stations, station by station, the same subjected sun, moon, stars by His command. The sun’s dia and moon’s noor recall the dawn breaker and the night for rest and the sun and moon travelling with precision of the Almighty, All-Knowing, showing the day-night overlap and the soon being. The ordaining, or “qaddarahu,” to measure, to decree with calculation, is the same qadar that wrote twelve months in the Record since creation and that measures the spoils’ fifth, the alms’ eight, the inheritance for arham, and the term for each community that cannot be delayed.\n\nThe purpose “so that you may know the number of years and calculation of time” or “li-ta‘lamoo adada al-sineena wa al-hisab” gives a practical, civilizational use, like the fertile land producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave for the thankful and the winds producing every fruit as sign for believers, showing creation serves both worship and worldly order.\n\nThe seal “Allah did not create all this except for a purpose. He makes the signs clear for people of knowledge” or “ma khalaqa Allahu thalika illa bil-haqqi yufassilu al-ayat li-qawmin ya‘lamoon” repeats “bil-haqqi,” with truth, purpose, the same truth that must be established even to the wicked’s dismay and the illusions that fail, the truth binding in Torah, Gospel, Quran, the prior decree and the noble Word fulfilled. “Yufassilu al-ayat,” He details signs, is the same varying, or “nusarrifu,” detailing for the thankful, the mindful, the knowledgeable.\n\nA common misunderstanding takes sun and moon as mere decoration. The verse says they are a dated system ordained for years and hisab, inviting those who do not expect to meet Us, pleased with this world and heedless of signs, to become mindful by looking at alternation of night and day and all He created in heavens and earth, truly signs for the mindful, before the life like rain mixed plants beautified then mown as if never flourished, and before Allah invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to the Straight Path.",
  "6": "“Surely in the alternation of the day and the night, and in all that Allah has created in the heavens and the earth, there are truly signs for those mindful ˹of Him˺” generalizes the sun and moon phases into the constant cycle. The previous verse detailed the sun radiant, moon reflected, with measured stations to know years and calculation, not created except with purpose and detailed for people of knowledge. This verse says “Surely in the alternation of the day and the night, and in all that Allah has created in the heavens and the earth, there are truly signs for those mindful of Him” or “inna fi ikhtilafi al-laili wa al-nahari wa ma khalaqa Allahu fi al-samawati wal-ardi la-ayatin li-qawmin yattaqoon.” “Ikhtilaf,” alternation, difference, succession, like the day and night overlapping in rapid succession, yughshi, covering, chasing swiftly, the same overlapping praised as “He makes day and night overlap.” The alternation is the daily rehearsal of the appointment where Allah showed them few in dream and made each appear few to the other, and where drowsiness gave serenity and rain purified, showing affair-conducting.\n\nThe second sign “and in all that Allah has created in the heavens and the earth” expands beyond the two luminaries to every leaf, grain, wind, spring, calf that neither speaks, fish on Sabbath, sacred months, all that is created from a single soul, the “ma khalaqa” whose knowledge encompasses everything and whose record wrote twelve months.\n\nThe audience “for those mindful of Him” or “li-qawmin yattaqoon” is the same mindful for whom the Home of the Hereafter is far better, the best clothing, the furqan for the mindful, the alms that are obligation, the earth belonging to Allah with outcome for the righteous, the sceptical who take parents as allies if they choose disbelief are the true wrongdoers, the opposite of the mindful whose hearts tremble, faith increases, trust, prayer, donation.\n\nThis links directly to the next diagnosis of those who do not expect to meet Us, pleased with this worldly life, heedless of Our signs, whose home will be Fire because of what they committed, versus those who believe and do good, guided through faith, rivers under feet in Gardens of Bliss, prayer Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise. The signs’ purpose is to make the heedless become mindful before the enjoyment of this world, just like rain producing mixture then mown as if never flourished, before Allah invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to the Straight Path.\n\nA common error reserves signs for scholars alone. The verse says signs are for the mindful, the grateful, the patient who see the valley, dream, double fewness, and know all matters return, inviting anyone whose heart is not sealed like those who even if they see every sign will not believe to become mindful by observing alternation.",
  "7": "“Indeed, those who do not expect to meet Us, being pleased and content with this worldly life, and who are heedless of Our signs” diagnoses the opposite of the mindful for whom alternation and creation are signs. The previous verse said in alternation and in all He created are signs for the mindful; this verse describes “Indeed, those who do not expect to meet Us” or “inna allatheena la yarjoona liqa’ana” - la yarjoona, not hoping, not expecting, the same liqa’ that losers denied until the Hour surprised them with burdens, that the barrier’s dialogue confirmed as true promise to both sides, and that the Heights hoped to enter. Not expecting to meet Us is the root of taking faith as play and being deluded by worldly life, for which today We forget them as they forgot the meeting, for which their deeds become void and ultimate disgrace is Hell home.\n\nThe second trait “being pleased and content with this worldly life” or “wa radoo bil-hayati al-dunya wa itma’annoo biha” is the same rida that the hypocrites sought from people swearing to please believers, but here they are pleased with dunya, the near lowly gain, the arad al-dunya, the small price for revelations, the hoarded gold to be heated, the reallocated sacred months to maintain number, the “This is what we deserve” in prosperity. “Itma’annoo biha,” to be at ease, to be tranquil with it, is the false sakeenah, the opposite of the true sakeenah sent upon Messenger and believers and in the cave, the false tranquillity that made the hypocrites feel secure while asleep or at play and made losers feel secure from planning.\n\nThe third trait “and who are heedless of Our signs” or “wa allatheena hum an ayatina ghafileen” is the heedless, or “ghafileen,” the cattle-like with hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, even less guided, entirely heedless, for whom Allah has turned hearts because they do not comprehend, who even if they see every sign will not believe, who prefer the crooked path, who are like those who deny signs and meeting whose deeds are vain, who are tried once or twice yearly yet neither repent nor learn.\n\nThis triple links directly to the next verse’s home is Fire because of what they committed, and to the contrast: those who believe and do good are guided through faith, rivers under feet in Gardens of Bliss, prayer Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise. A common misunderstanding treats not expecting the meeting as private opinion. The Quran shows it produces pleased, tranquil worldliness and heedlessness of signs, the infertile hardly producing despite the sun radiant and moon phases measured, the enjoyment little compared to Hereafter that clings heavily to land. The remedy is to expect the meeting, like the mindful who keep prayer and pay zakat and fear none but Allah, whose building is on taqwa and ridwan, not on the crumbling cliff.",
  "8": "“they will have the Fire as a home because of what they have committed” states the recompense for those who do not expect to meet Us, pleased with this world, heedless of Our signs. The previous verse diagnosed that triple: not hoping for the meeting, pleased with dunya, heedless of signs. This verse says “they will have the Fire as a home because of what they have committed” or “ula’ika ma’wahumu al-naru bima kanu yaksiboon.” “Ma’wahumu al-nar,” home is the Fire, is the same home that is Hell, evil destination, for those who turn backs without manoeuvre, the bed of Hell with flames as cover for the wicked whose large numbers and arrogance are of no use, the everlasting stay sufficient for hypocrites and disbelievers, condemned, never-ending, the ultimate disgrace for opposing, the painful punishment for hurting the Messenger, the disgrace for apes, the branding for hoarders, the torment intended through wealth and children in this world before souls depart while disbelievers, the home of the rebellious shown. Home here is “ma’wa,” refuge, abode, ironically the refuge the hypocrites would rush to if they could find any hiding-place headlong, but the only refuge they find is Fire.\n\nThe reason “because of what they have committed” or “bima kanu yaksiboon” uses “kasab,” to earn, to acquire, the same earning that makes the scale heavy or light, that piles evil to cast, true losers, that becomes void when wading as those before them, that makes the worst beings persist never to have faith, that will be observed by Allah, Messenger, and believers and informed by the Knower, that is never discounted for the good-doers but is void for deniers.\n\nThis links the heedless Fire home to the contrast in the next verse: those who believe and do good, guided through faith, rivers under feet in Gardens of Bliss, prayer Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise, showing two homes built on two foundations: one on taqwa and ridwan, like the mosque founded on righteousness from the first day, the other on the crumbling cliff that tumbles into Fire, the dog panting whether chased or left, the cattle even less guided.\n\nA common error imagines Fire as arbitrary. The verse says it is because of what they committed, the same hands that withheld after vow, that consumed wealth wrongfully, hindered, reallocated months, clung to land, preferred the little enjoyment, said “We heard but do not listen,” had hearts not understanding, even if they see every sign would not believe, and chose disbelief, whose hearts were sealed. For the mindful for whom alternation and creation are signs, the Home is not Fire but the Home of Peace to which Allah invites and guides whom He wills to the Straight Path, the finest reward and more, with no gloom or disgrace covering faces, residents of Paradise forever, the opposite of humiliation covering the evil-doers as if faces covered with patches of night.",
  "9": "“Surely those who believe and do good, their Lord will guide them ˹to Paradise˺ through their faith, rivers will flow under their feet in the Gardens of Bliss” gives the counter-home to the Fire as home for the heedless who do not expect the meeting. The previous verse said they will have Fire because of what they committed; this verse says “Surely those who believe and do good, their Lord will guide them to Paradise through their faith, rivers will flow under their feet in the Gardens of Bliss” or “inna allatheena amanoo wa amiloo al-salihati yahdeehim Rabbuhum bi-imanihim tajri min tahtihimu al-anharu fi jannati al-na‘eem.” The opening “Those who believe and do good” is the same true believers whose hearts tremble, faith increases, trust, establish prayer, donate, the guardians of one another who encourage good, forbid evil, establish, pay, obey, shown mercy, Almighty, All-Wise, the same sellers whose lives and wealth are purchased for Paradise ultimate triumph, the same repentant, worshipping, praising, fasting, bowing, prostrating, enjoining, forbidding, limit-observing, given good news.\n\n“their Lord will guide them to Paradise through their faith” or “yahdeehim Rabbuhum bi-imanihim” shows guidance as the staff that devours, the sea that saves, the rain that purifies, the hearts united, the sakeenah at Hunain and cave, the furqan for the mindful. Faith itself becomes the light that lets dead hearts walk among people, the same guiding ummah, as earlier “He has guided some while others are destined to stray because they took devils as masters thinking they are guided,” and later “Allah invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to the Straight Path.”\n\nThe image “rivers will flow under their feet in the Gardens of Bliss” or “tajri min tahtihimu al-anharu fi jannati al-na‘eem” is the same rivers where bitterness removed, the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, the honourable provision, the great reward forever, the everlasting bliss with mercy, pleasure, and ultimate triumph for Emigrants, Helpers, followers in goodness, Allah pleased with them.\n\nA common misunderstanding separates faith from guidance to Paradise. The verse says Lord will guide them through faith, as earlier the fertile land produces abundantly by its Lord’s leave for the thankful, the same Lord who wrote twelve months and made four sacred as Right Way, whose creation and command are His, blessed, whose most beautiful names are to be called upon, kept away from abusers, with guidance Establishing justice. The contrast is the Fire home for the heedless who were pleased with dunya and heedless of signs, whose enjoyment is like rain mixed plants beautified then mown as if never flourished, inviting the mindful to choose faith-led guidance to Bliss.",
  "10": "“in which their prayer will be, ‘Glory be to You, O Allah!’ and their greeting will be, ‘Peace!’ and their closing prayer will be, ‘All praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds!’” details the speech inside the Gardens of Bliss where faith guides and rivers flow under feet. The previous verse promised that guidance; this verse lets us hear it. The phrase “in which their prayer will be, ‘Glory be to You, O Allah!’ and their greeting will be, ‘Peace!’ and their closing prayer will be, ‘All praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds!’” or “da‘wahum fiha subhanaka Allahumma wa tahiyyatuhum fiha salam wa akhiru da‘wahum ani alhamdu lillahi Rabbi al-alameen” uses three utterances that have threaded the entire narrative: “Subhanaka,” Glory be to You, like Moses after the mountain levelled and the angels nearest glorifying, the same glorification above what they associate when good offspring were granted and jinn were associated though He created them. “Salam,” Peace, like the Heights’ “Peace be upon you,” the Home of Peace to which Allah invites, the greeting of the worthy, the same salam that the warner’s sakeenah brings. “Alhamdu lillahi Rabbi al-alameen,” All praise is for Allah, Lord of all worlds, is the same praise that closes the Book’s opening, that follows the uprooting of wrongdoers, that was after gardens and the noble Word fulfilled, and that is the closing of the Gardens’ prayer, uniting the Lord of the two-thousand-angel Badr and the cave’s second-of-two.\n\nThe sequence shows the dwellers’ constant remembrance, the opposite of the heedless who are pleased with dunya, not expecting the meeting, whose home is Fire because of what they committed, whose wealth and children torment, who reallocate months as increase in disbelief, who hoard and whose treasure brands, who mock and whose hearts are turned because they do not comprehend. In the Gardens, prayer begins with glorification, social life is greeting of peace, and closure is praise, the same remembrance commanded as “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and not be among the heedless” and “When the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy.”\n\nA common error imagines Paradise as silent enjoyment. The verse shows it as speaking, glorifying, greeting, praising, the same speaking that the calf could not do, the idols have no feet, hands, eyes, ears to do, the same speaking that the mindful do when surah revealed increases faith and they rejoice. The closing praise mirrors the earlier praise for uprooting wrongdoers and for sending winds, inviting the astonishing who called the warner magician to move from astonishment to glorification, from heedlessness to mindful signs, from brief enjoyment like rain mixed then mown, to the invitation to Home of Peace He guides whom He wills.",
  "11": "“If Allah were to hasten evil for people as they wish to hasten good, they would have certainly been doomed. But We leave those who do not expect to meet Us to wander blindly in their defiance” follows the Gardens’ glorification, peace, and praise with a reminder about hastening. The phrase “If Allah were to hasten evil for people as they wish to hasten good, they would have certainly been doomed” or “wa law yu‘ajjilu Allahu li-nasi al-sharra isti‘jalahum bil-khairi la-qudiya ilaihim ajaluhum” uses “isti‘jal,” to hasten, the same hastening as the polytheists who prayed “Rain stones if this is truth,” who challenged “Bring what you threaten if you are truthful,” who said “Had we been able we would have joined” and whose near gain would have been followed but long distance seemed too long, and as earlier “They ask ‘Why has no sign been sent down’ Say the unseen is with Allah so wait.” The counterfactual shows mercy in delay: if evil were hastened as good is wished hastened, the term, or “ajal,” that cannot be delayed would have already been concluded, as earlier every community has a term that when it comes cannot be delayed, and the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend would have ended. “La-qudiya ilaihim ajaluhum,” their term would have been decreed against them, mirrors the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful, and the hour too tremendous and will take by surprise.\n\nThe second clause “But We leave those who do not expect to meet Us to wander blindly in their defiance” or “fa-natharu allatheena la yarjoona liqa’ana fi tughyanihim ya‘mahoona” is the same “yahoolu baina al-mar’i wa qalbihi” and “leave them to wander blindly in their defiance” as earlier “Whoever Allah allows to stray none can guide, leaving them to wander,” the same heedless like cattle even less guided, entirely heedless, with hearts not understanding, whose surah increases wickedness. Leaving to wander is the other face of sending sakeenah to the mindful, uniting hearts, and promising Gardens where prayer is Glory, greeting Peace, closing Praise.\n\nA common misunderstanding demands immediate punishment for evil to prove justice. The verse says immediate hastening would doom, as earlier famines, plagues, quakes, and drownings were spaced as tests once or twice yearly yet they neither repented nor learned, and the hypocrites’ hearts were turned because they do not comprehend. Delay is mercy and test, the flawless planning that gives rope, where wealth and children can become torment or, if goodness is found, better and forgiveness, and where the mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted can still be with the truthful.",
  "12": "“Whenever someone is touched by hardship, they cry out to Us, whether lying on their side, sitting, or standing. But when We relieve their hardship, they return to their old ways as if they had never cried to Us to remove any hardship! This is how the misdeeds of the transgressors have been made appealing to them” diagnoses the hastening pattern just described as human inconsistency, linking the “We leave those who do not expect to meet Us to wander” to its lived behaviour. The phrase “Whenever someone is touched by hardship, they cry out to Us, whether lying on their side, sitting, or standing” or “wa itha massa al-insana al-durru da‘ana li-janbihi aw qa‘idan aw qa’iman” uses “massa,” to touch, the same touching that afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine so they might remember and that made winds bring heavy clouds, now touching a person with “durr,”, harm, the same harm of tight chest as if climbing into the sky, earth closing despite vastness, and the wind that makes ships gale and waves overwhelm where they assume they are doomed and cry “If You save us we will be grateful.” The three postures cover all states, lying, sitting, standing, showing hardship makes even the heedless remember, as earlier the Valley’s near and far and caravan below made them cry for help and be answered with a thousand angels.\n\nThe reversal “But when We relieve their hardship, they return to their old ways as if they had never cried to Us to remove any hardship!” or “fa-lamma kashafna anhu durrahu marra ka-an lam yad‘una ila durrin massahu” mirrors the idolaters who, when rescued from darkness on land and sea, associate, and the hypocrites who, when torment removed until term they must reach, break, and the Sabbath town who forgot what they were reminded, and the hoarders who withheld after vow. “Ka-an lam yad‘u,” as if never called, is the heedless cattle with ears not hearing, the worst deaf and dumb, the dog panting whether chased or left, the infertile hardly producing despite rain.\n\nThe seal “This is how the misdeeds of the transgressors have been made appealing to them” or “kathalika zuyyina lil-musrifeen ma kanu ya‘maloon” uses “zuyyina,” beautified, as earlier Satan beautified evil deeds, each people’s deeds made appealing, the sacred months’ reallocating beautified. The transgressors, or “musrifeen,” those who exceed bounds, like the wasteful who were told “Eat without waste, He does not like the wasteful,” the reallocators, the hoarders, the oath swearing never said while uttering blasphemy, have their returning-to-old-ways made appealing.\n\nA common error sees this as another’s problem. The verse says it is the human touched by hardship who, when relieved, returns, inviting the mindful to make the relief a lasting gratitude, the small or large donation written for best reward, the valley crossed, the remembrance morning and evening without heedlessness, rather than the beautiful return to forgetfulness.",
  "13": "“We surely destroyed ˹other˺ peoples before you when they did wrong, and their messengers came to them with clear proofs but they would not believe! This is how We reward the wicked people” universalizes the hardship-relief forgetting into history. The previous verse showed the individual who when hardship touches cries in every posture, but when relieved returns as if never called, his transgression beautified. This verse says “We surely destroyed other peoples before you when they did wrong, and their messengers came to them with clear proofs but they would not believe! This is how We reward the wicked people” or “wa laqad ahlakna al-qurona min qablikum lamma zalamoo wa ja’athum rusuluhum bil-bayyinat wa ma kanoo li-yu’mino.” Peoples, or “quron,” generations, like Noah, ’Ad, Thamud, Abraham’s people, Midian, Lot’s overturned cities, Pharaoh and those before him whose wont was to disbelieve in signs so Allah seized them. “Lamma zalamoo,” when they did wrong, placed things where they do not belong, like whistling prayer, spending to hinder, taking rabbis as lords, hoarding, reallocating months, and saying “We will be forgiven” while seizing. Messengers with clear proofs, or “bil-bayyinat,” mirrors Moses’ staff-snake and shining hand, Salih’s she-camel, Hud’s stature reminder, Noah’s tremendous Day warning, Shu‘ayb’s just measure, the surahs that increase faith for believers and wickedness for the sick, the Book explained with knowledge, the mountain raised like a cloud.\n\nThe verdict “They would not believe! This is how We reward the wicked people” or “wa ma kanoo li-yu’mino”... “kathalika najzi al-qawma al-mujrimeen” uses “mujrimeen,” the wicked who cut off, whose end the watcher is told to see, whose building on the crumbling cliff tumbles, whose deeds become void, whose evil is piled and cast, true losers. The reward is destruction, or “ahlakna,” the same “We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend, I delay but My planning is flawless” and the Hour too tremendous, their term would have been decreed if evil hastened.\n\nThis connects to the next “Then We made you their successors in the land to see how you would act,” making the destroyed generations the predecessors of the current successors who inherit the land after them, the same succession after former residents that tests, the same “We dispersed them in groups and tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return.”\n\nA common misunderstanding treats past destruction as irrelevant. The verse says their messengers came with clear proofs but they would not believe, the same proof now with the Quran that is insight, guide, and mercy whose recitation should be heard attentively and silently with mercy, whose remembrance morning and evening keeps from heedlessness, whose mosque founded on righteousness is more worthy. The wicked’s reward today is the same if they persist, while the mindful who firmly abide have Gardens and ultimate triumph.",
  "14": "“Then We made you their successors in the land to see how you would act” follows the destruction of former wicked generations who would not believe despite clear proofs, placing the present community in their seats. The phrase “Then We made you their successors in the land to see how you would act” or “thumma ja‘alnakum khala’ifa fil-ardi min ba‘dihim li-nanzura kaifa ta‘maloon” uses “khala’ifa,” successors, inheritors, the same successors after Noah, after ’Ad, after Thamud, after Pharaoh’s drowning when the oppressed became successors of eastern and western blessed lands, after the dispersion in groups where some were righteous others less so, after the calm “We have established you on earth and provided livelihood yet you seldom give thanks” and the mountain raised like a cloud to hold firmly. The land, or “ard,” is the same earth belonging to Allah to grant to whom He chooses and outcome for the righteous, the same earth that closed at Hunain and for the three, the same earth where twelve months are ordained and four sacred, the same land after the mountain covenant should not be wronged in.\n\nThe purpose “to see how you would act” or “li-nanzura kaifa ta‘maloon” is observation, testing, the same testing that counts every thirst, fatigue, hunger, tread, loss, donation, wadi crossing as written for best reward, the same ranks according to what they did and the weighing just where heavy succeed and light doom themselves. Seeing how you act distinguishes the fertile who produce abundantly by the Lord’s leave for the thankful from the infertile who hardly produce, the true believers who establish prayer and donate from the hoarders who withhold and whose treasure will brand, the mindful who will be with the truthful from the rebellious whose hearts are sealed.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next where Our clear revelations are recited, those who do not expect to meet Us say “Bring us a different Quran or make some changes,” the same change as “Hittah” gate changers and Sabbath fish tricksters and sacred month reallocators, all acts being observed, and to the apostolic “It is not for me to change it on my own, I only follow what is revealed, I fear the tremendous Day” and “Had Allah willed I would not have recited nor made it known, I lived a whole life among you, do you not understand?”\n\nA common error views succession as entitlement. The Quran frames it as examination, the same examination that wealth and children are test and beloveds more dear test and sacred months test, where the best clothing is to be mindful and with the truthful. For successors who were “right to hope will be among the truly guided” if they believe, pray, pay, fear none but Allah, this is the moment to act, before the Hour tremendous and only surprise arrives.",
  "15": "“When Our clear revelations are recited to them, those who do not expect to meet Us say ˹to the Prophet˺, ‘Bring us a different Quran or make some changes in it.’ Say ˹to them˺, ‘It is not for me to change it on my own. I only follow what is revealed to me. I fear, if I were to disobey my Lord, the punishment of a tremendous Day’” shows the first act of successors under observation. The phrase “When Our clear revelations are recited to them, those who do not expect to meet Us say to the Prophet, ‘Bring us a different Quran or make some changes in it’” or “wa itha tutla alaihim ayatuna bayyinat qala allatheena la yarjoona liqa’ana’ti bi-Qur’anin ghairi hatha aw baddilhu” repeats the triad “those who do not expect to meet Us, pleased with worldly life, heedless of Our signs” whose home will be Fire, whose enjoyment is like rain mixed plants beautified then mown. “Bayyinat,” clear, the same bayyinat that made messengers come with clear proofs but the wicked would not believe and this is how the wicked are rewarded, now rejected as insufficient, as earlier “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables” and “No matter what sign you bring to deceive us we will never believe” and Pharaoh’s “skilled magician” and the chiefs’ “fool, liar, misguided.” The demand “Bring a different Quran or make some changes” is the same changing as “Hittah” at the gate, reallocating sacred months while maintaining number, taking rabbis as lords to permit what Allah forbade, like desiring a god like theirs after the sea.\n\nThe reply “Say to them, ‘It is not for me to change it on my own. I only follow what is revealed to me. I fear, if I were to disobey my Lord, the punishment of a tremendous Day’” or “qul ma yakoonoo li an ubaddilahu min tilqa’i nafsi in attabi‘u illa ma yooha ilayya inne akhafu in asaitu Rabbi athaba yawmin azeem” repeats the creed “I only follow what is revealed to me” from the Book explained with knowledge to warn, the same following that defines the ear of good who believes in Allah and has faith in believers and is mercy. Fear of tremendous Day, or “athaba yawmin azeem,” is the same tremendous Day Noah feared for his people, the same Day fulfilment when those who ignored will say messengers came with truth and seek intercessors, and the same Day whose weight is heavy for heavens and earth and will take by surprise.\n\nThis links succession’s test to preservation: the successor who demands a different Quran is the infertile that hardly produces, the dog panting, the cattle even less guided, the worst deaf and dumb, while the fertile holds firmly to the Book and takes the best of it, listens attentively and silently so mercy is shown, remembers inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness, as the angels nearest are not too proud to glorify and prostrate, inviting the astonishing who called the warner magician to move from astonishment to the honourable status with the Lord that the warner promised.",
  "16": "“Say, ‘Had Allah willed, I would not have recited it to you, nor would He have made it known to you. I had lived my whole life among you before this ˹revelation˺. Do you not understand?’” continues the Prophet’s defence after refusing to bring a different Quran or make changes. The previous verse quoted the demand of those who do not expect the meeting, “Bring a different Quran or make some changes,” and his reply “It is not for me to change it on my own, I only follow what is revealed, I fear the tremendous Day.” This verse adds the historical proof: “Say, ‘Had Allah willed, I would not have recited it to you, nor would He have made it known to you. I had lived my whole life among you before this revelation. Do you not understand?’” or “qul law shaa’a Allahu ma talawtuhu alaikum wa la adrakum bihi fa-qad labithtu feekum umran min qablihi a-fa-la ta‘qiloon.” The first clause “Had Allah willed, I would not have recited” places recitation under divine will, the same will that made the valley appointment, the dream few, the double fewness, the winds with heavy clouds, the sending of sakeenah, the uniting of hearts when all riches could not, the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful and good, and the Hour’s heaviness. Recitation is not self-authored like the golden calf’s lowing or the hoarded gold’s branding, but willed.\n\nThe second clause “nor would He have made it known to you” shows making known, or “adrakum,” is also His act, like making the Book insight, guide, and mercy for those who believe, and like making the alms for eight as obligation.\n\nThe personal evidence “I had lived my whole life among you before this revelation. Do you not understand?” or “fa-qad labithtu feekum umran min qablihi a-fa-la ta‘qiloon” appeals to the long labth, stay, a whole lifetime, or “umran,” among them before revelation, the same forty years before the call where he was known as truthful, not a poet or magician, the same “We have sent revelation to a man from among themselves” astonishment answered by “no, he is not insane, he is a clear warner” and “there has come to you a messenger from among yourselves, concerned, anxious, gracious, merciful.” A whole life without poetry suddenly bringing the Quran rich in wisdom, whose verses are of the wise Book that splits seeds and levels mountains, proves it is not his to change.\n\nA common misunderstanding reduces the Quran to the Prophet’s literature. The lifetime argument shows the Book explained with knowledge, whose most beautiful names are to be called upon, whose creation and command are His, blessed, whose light He perfects even to disbelievers’ dismay, is not his to alter, as earlier the worthiest warner fears the tremendous Day if he disobeyed. For the mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, this invites understanding, or “ta‘qiloon,” to see the lifetime as the greatest witness that the recitation is from the All-Hearing, All-Knowing whose promise is always true to originate then resurrect to reward justly.",
  "17": "“Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations? Indeed, the wicked will never succeed” follows the Prophet’s lifetime proof that he only follows what is revealed. The question “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations?” or “fa-man azlamu mimman iftara ala Allahi kathiban aw kathaba bi-ayatihi” uses “azlam,” more wrong, more unjust, the same greatest wrong asked about associating without sultan, taking rabbis as lords, and fabricating shares for associates. Fabrication, or “iftara,” includes “Bring a different Quran or make some changes,” inventing a different revelation, and denial, or “kathaba bi-ayatihi,” includes saying “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables,” rejecting the clear bayyinat that made messengers come but the wicked would not believe. The verse mirrors the earlier “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah” and the “Evil indeed is what they have done” for choosing small price over revelations.\n\nThe seal “Indeed, the wicked will never succeed” or “innahu la yuflihu al-mujrimoon” repeats the ultimate loss, the same “They are the true losers” whose deeds are void, whose share was enjoyed as those before them superior in might, whose stories of Noah and the overturned cities were received, and whose wicked reward is destruction, the building on crumbling cliff that tumbles with them into Fire, the hoarded treasure heated to brand.\n\nSuccess, or “falah,” is the same success as the Gardens where prayer is Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise, where the weighing just makes heavy succeed and light doom themselves, where the barrier’s Heights recognize and enter. The wicked’s non-success contrasts with “It is they who will be successful” for the Messenger and believers who strived with wealth and lives will have all the best and the foremost pleased, whose building is on taqwa and ridwan.\n\nA common misunderstanding views fabricating or denying as intellectual freedom. The Quran frames it as the greatest zulm because it makes the worst beings, deaf and dumb who do not understand, the heedless like cattle, the dog panting whether chased or left, the evil example, the losers who feel secure from planning, and whose wealth and children are worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers. For the successors made successors to see how they would act, the choice between fabricating a different Quran and holding firmly to the Book where reward is never discounted determines whether the successor is among the mindful granted furqan or among the hoarders and reallocators.",
  "18": "“They worship besides Allah others who can neither harm nor benefit them, and say, ‘These are our intercessors with Allah.’ Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Are you informing Allah of something He does not know in the heavens or the earth? Glorified and Exalted is He above what they associate ˹with Him˺!’” dismantles the intercession claim that follows the greatest wrong. The opening “They worship besides Allah others who can neither harm nor benefit them” or “wa ya‘budoona min dooni Allahi ma la yadurruhum wa la yanfa‘uhum” repeats the audit of idols that cannot create, are created, cannot help themselves or others, cannot hear guidance, are servants like you with no feet, hands, eyes, ears, facing you but not seeing, the same lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides, the same whistling prayer. “La yadurruhum wa la yanfa‘uhum” neither harm nor benefit, mirrors the “If you call them to guidance they cannot hear and you may see them facing but they cannot see” and the earlier “Do they have feet... hands... eyes... ears?”\n\nThe claim “These are our intercessors with Allah” or “ha’ula’i shufa‘a’una inda Allah” is the intercession without permission that the previous chapter said “None can intercede except by His permission,” whose permission is for the truth-guiding community, the hopeful on the Heights, the repentant, the limit-observing. Claiming intercessors without permission is the same lordship taken besides One God, Glorified above what they associate.\n\nThe challenge “Are you informing Allah of something He does not know in the heavens or the earth? Glorified and Exalted is He above what they associate with Him!” or “a-tunabbi’oona Allaha bima la ya‘lamu fi al-samawati wa la fil-ardi subhanahu wa ta‘ala amma yushrikoon” uses “tunabbi’oon,” to inform, to tell, the same informing that Allah will give a full account with sure knowledge for We were never absent and that you will be returned to the Knower who will inform you. Informing Allah of an intercessor He does not know shows the intercessor is invented, like the shares for associates by claim and the Sabbath fish abundance on forbidden day.\n\nThe seal “Glorified and Exalted is He above what they associate” or “subhanahu wa ta‘ala amma yushrikoon” is the same glorification above what they associate when good offspring were granted and jinn were associated, and after the mountain was levelled and Moses glorified. A common error imagines intercession via helpless idols secures help. The Quran says the idols cannot harm nor benefit, cannot be known to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who wrote twelve months in the Record and who stands between person and heart. For the heedless pleased with dunya and the mindful who see signs, the choice is to call upon Him by the most beautiful names and keep away from those who abuse them, and to be the group that guides with truth and establishes justice where intercession by permission remains a mercy, not a right.",
  "19": "“Humanity was once nothing but a single community ˹of believers˺, but then they differed. Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord, their differences would have been settled ˹at once˺” explains the origin of division that makes intercessor claims plausible to the misled. The phrase “Humanity was once nothing but a single community of believers, but then they differed” or “wa ma kana al-nasu illa ummatan wahidatan fa-ikhtalafoo” echoes the single soul from which you were created then made spouse for tranquility, the primordial testimony “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify,” and the mountain covenant, all pointing to one fitrah community, one straight path that the Book makes clear so perhaps they will return. Differing, or “ikhtalafoo,” is the same differing that made humanity split after the single community, that made the fate of Pharaoh’s wont, the worst beings violate every time, and that made the building on the crumbling cliff divide, the same differing that the twelve-month Record and the four sacred aim to order.\n\nThe second clause “Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord, their differences would have been settled at once” or “wa la-wa la kalimatun sabaqat min Rabbika la-qudiya bainahum fima fihi yakhtalifoon” refers to the “kalimah sabaqat,” a word, decree that preceded, like the prior decree that made spoils lawful and good and spared tremendous punishment, the prior decree that wrote the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, the prior decree that made enjoyment lawful, the prior decree that delays the Hour though it is too tremendous and will only take by surprise. Without that decree, the difference between the calf worshippers’ lowing and the mindful’s glorification would have been judged immediately, as “If Allah were to hasten evil as they hasten good, they would be doomed.”\n\nThis verse links the astonishment at the human warner—warn humanity and give good news that believers will have honourable status—to the human community’s initial unity, showing the warner is sent to reunite what differed, the same warner who is not insane but a clear warner, whose surah increases faith for believers and wickedness for the sick, whose Book is insight, guide, mercy.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats difference as natural pluralism needing no guidance. The Quran says difference after the single community is a deviation that the prior decree delays judging, allowing the spacious to be opened and the tight to wander, the fertile to produce and the infertile to hardly produce, until the term that cannot be delayed brings the weighing just, the barrier, the Fire home for the heedless and the Gardens where prayer is Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise for the faithful.",
  "20": "“They ask, ‘Why has no ˹other˺ sign been sent down to him from his Lord?’ Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘˹The knowledge of˺ the unseen is with Allah alone. So wait! I too am waiting with you’” returns to the demanded sign that follows the single community’s differing. The question “They ask, ‘Why has no other sign been sent down to him from his Lord?’” or “wa yaqooloona law-la unzila alaihi ayatun min Rabbihi” is the same demand as “Bring us a different Quran or make some changes,” the same “No matter what sign you bring to deceive us we will never believe,” the same Pharaoh’s “skilled magician who seeks to drive you from land” and “If you have come with a sign bring it if you are truthful,” and the same Quraysh’s “We have heard, we could produce similar, it is ancient fables” and “Rain stones if this is truth.” The demanded sign is an “ayah,” a sign like the staff-snake, shining hand, she-camel not to be harmed, twelve springs, shaded clouds, manna, or the mountain raised like a cloud, but on their terms.\n\nThe answer “Say, O Prophet, ‘The knowledge of the unseen is with Allah alone. So wait! I too am waiting with you’” or “fa-qul innama al-ghaybu lillahi fa-intaziroo innee ma‘akum mina al-muntazireen” places the unseen, or “ghaib,” with Allah alone, the same unseen whose keys none knows except Him, whose Record wrote twelve months since creation, whose knowledge is between person and heart, whose Hour is too tremendous and will take by surprise. Waiting, or “intaziroo,” is the same waiting as “Then wait, I too am waiting with you” after the river appointment, the same “Are you awaiting except one of the two best things? We await affliction,” the same “You will soon know who will fare best,” the same “Keep waiting, we too are waiting” with the disbelievers. Both sides wait, but one waits for the unseen to be revealed as mercy, the other waits for a sign to disbelieve.\n\nThis links to the next where, when people are given a taste of mercy after hardship, they swiftly devise plots against revelations, and Allah is swifter in devising, His angels record what you devise, showing waiting is not idle but morally active.\n\nA common error thinks not receiving a demanded sign proves the warner false. The Quran says the warner is not the treasurer of the unseen’s signs to produce on demand; he only follows what is revealed and fears the tremendous Day. The unseen with Allah includes the Hour whose weight is heavy, the winds that produce heavy clouds, the fertile barren, the twelve springs, and the term that cannot be delayed, so the proper stance is to wait with the mindful, whose building is on taqwa and ridwan, whose prayer when the Quran is recited is listening attentively and silently so mercy is shown.",
  "21": "“When We give people a taste of mercy after being afflicted with a hardship, they swiftly devise plots against Our revelations! Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Allah is swifter in devising ˹punishment˺. Surely Our messenger-angels record whatever you devise’” shows what the waiters do while waiting for the unseen. The condition “When We give people a taste of mercy after being afflicted with a hardship, they swiftly devise plots against Our revelations!” or “wa itha athaqna al-nasa rahmatan min ba‘di darra’a massat hum itha lahum makrun fi ayatina” repeats the cycle “We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine so they might remember, in prosperity they said ‘This is what we deserve,’ in adversity they blamed Moses,” and the “We tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return,” and the Sabbath fish abundant on forbidden day, and the “We dispersed them in groups tested with prosperity and adversity.” The mercy after hardship, or “rahma min ba‘di darra,” could be rain after famine, health after illness, safe return after the valley’s fear, or the reassurance and unseen forces after Hunain’s earth closed. Plotting, or “makr,” against revelations is the same elegant, beautified deception that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter, that makes the Way seem crooked, that hoarders use and reallocators use as increase in disbelief, and that the worst beings use to violate every time.\n\nThe reply “Say, O Prophet, ‘Allah is swifter in devising punishment. Surely Our messenger-angels record whatever you devise’” or “qul Allahu asra‘u makran inna rusulana yak tub oona ma tamkuroon” uses “asra‘u makran,” swifter in planning, the flawless planning that gives rope in ways they cannot comprehend, whose prior decree makes the Hour heavy and surprise, whose creation in six Days and Throne and conducting every affair, whose winds send heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit similarly raising the dead, all prove His swiftness exceeds their swiftness. The recorders, or “rusulana yak tub oona,” messenger-angels who write, are the same who were told “I am with you, make believers firm, I will cast horror,” and the same who take souls beating faces and backs to taste burning, and the same who never absent when giving a full account with sure knowledge, whose record wrote twelve months.\n\nA common misunderstanding counts the mercy taste as approval of plotting. The verse shows the taste is a test, like wealth and children fitnah, like beloveds more dear, where the infertile hardly produces and the heedless increase in wickedness upon wickedness when a surah is revealed. The swift planning that records every devise ensures the piling of evil to cast into Hell, true losers, while the mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer find their donation small or large and valley crossing written for best reward.",
  "22": "“He is the One Who enables you to travel through land and sea. And it so happens that you are on ships, sailing with a favourable wind, to the passengers’ delight. Suddenly, the ships are overcome by a gale wind and those on board are overwhelmed by waves from every side, and they assume they are doomed. They cry out to Allah ˹alone˺ in sincere devotion, ‘If You save us from this, we will certainly be grateful’” illustrates the mercy-hardship-plotting cycle with a voyage that every listener knows. The opening “He is the One Who enables you to travel through land and sea” or “huwa allathee yusabbirukum fil-barri wa al-bahr” attributes both land and sea travel, the same “to Him belongs whatever is in day and night” and the “Winds ushering His mercy, heavy clouds driven to lifeless land producing every fruit” that are subjected by His command, blessed. The scene “And it so happens that you are on ships, sailing with a favourable wind, to the passengers’ delight. Suddenly, the ships are overcome by a gale wind and those on board are overwhelmed by waves from every side, and they assume they are doomed” describes delight turning to dread, the same delight that made the arrogant leave homes to be seen and the same dread that made the hypocrites fear a surah will expose hearts, the same waves that earlier enabled travel now become walls, as the earth despite vastness closed at Hunain and for the three. “Assume they are doomed” or “zannoo annahum uheeta bihim” is the same encompassing, or “uhita,” that Hell will engulf disbelievers and that Allah is Fully Aware of what they do.\n\nThe response “They cry out to Allah alone in sincere devotion, ‘If You save us from this, we will certainly be grateful’” or “da‘awu Allaha mukhliseena lahu al-deen la-in anjaitana min hathihi la-nakoonanna min al-shakireen” uses “mukhliseena lahu al-deen,” sincere devotion, the same ikhlas commanded as “devoted to worship, calling upon Him with sincere devotion” and as the best clothing of righteousness, the same gratefulness vowed when the light burden grew heavy “If You grant us good offspring we will be grateful” and when sailing “If You save us we will be grateful.” The sincerity is temporary, as the next verse will show, but it proves fitrah knows the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in hearts.\n\nThis links to the next “But as soon as He rescues them, they transgress in the land unjustly. O humanity, your transgression is only against your own souls. There is only brief enjoyment in this worldly life, then to Us is your return, then We will inform you” and to the earlier “There are some who pretend to listen but veils cast” and “If We give mercy after hardship they swiftly devise plots.” A common error trusts sincere prayer only in storm. The verse shows storm prayer is true, but lasting gratitude, like the fertile land producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave, is the test that must continue after the gale, lest the life like rain mixed plants beautified then mown as if never flourished become the home of those who were promised the Fire because of what they committed.",
  "23": "“But as soon as He rescues them, they transgress in the land unjustly. O humanity! Your transgression is only against your own souls. ˹There is only˺ brief enjoyment in this worldly life, then to Us is your return, and then We will inform you of what you used to do” completes the voyage’s lesson. The previous verse showed travellers in sincere devotion when gale and waves overwhelm, vowing “If You save us we will be grateful,” the same “We will certainly be grateful” of the pregnant pair. This verse says “But as soon as He rescues them, they transgress in the land unjustly” or “fa-lamma anjahum itha hum yabghoona fil-ardi bi-ghairi al-haqq.” The transgression, or “yabghoona,” exceeding bounds without right, is the same baghy that makes deeds void, that makes the building on the crumbling cliff tumble, that is the true prohibition “open and secret indecencies, sin, unjust aggression, shirk without sultan, saying about Allah what you do not know.” Injustly, or “bi-ghairi al-haqq,” without truth, mirrors “Allah did not create except with truth” and the light He perfects even to disbelievers’ dismay. The rescue that should have produced thankfulness, like rain producing every fruit and the Twelve springs and the manna and quails, instead produces the infertile hardly producing, the dog panting whether chased or left, the hoarders’ withholding and the reallocators’ increase.\n\nThe address “O humanity! Your transgression is only against your own souls. There is only brief enjoyment in this worldly life, then to Us is your return, and then We will inform you of what you used to do” or “ya ayyuha al-nasu innama baghyukum ala anfusikum mata‘u al-hayati al-dunya thumma ilaina marji‘ukum fa-nunabbi’ukum bima kuntum ta‘maloon” universalizes: “baghyukum ala anfusikum,” your excess harms yourselves, as earlier “They did not wrong Us but wronged themselves” after provision and “They only wronged their own souls” for the evil example whose deeds became void. Brief enjoyment, or “mata‘u al-hayati al-dunya,” is the little, qaleel, compared to everlasting Gardens with splendid homes and greatest pleasure, the little laugh that will be wept much, the near lowly gain seized claiming “We will be forgiven.” The return, or “ilaina marji‘ukum,” is the same gathering to the Knower who will inform you, where the weighing will be just, the barrier stands, and the Fire is home for the heedless.\n\nA common misunderstanding views transgression after rescue as harming others only. The verse says it harms your own souls, as earlier wealth and children as worldly torment before souls depart while disbelievers and building fuelled hypocrisy until hearts are torn. For the mindful for whom alternation and creation are signs, rescue should lead to the Home of Peace to which Allah invites and guides whom He wills, not to the brief enjoyment that closes the earth despite vastness.",
  "24": "“The life of this world is just like rain We send down from the sky, producing a mixture of plants which humans and animals consume. Then just as the earth looks its best, perfectly beautified, and its people think they have full control over it, there comes to it Our command by night or by day, so We mow it down as if it never flourished yesterday! This is how We make the signs clear for people who reflect” gives the parable that explains why transgression after rescue is self-harm and brief enjoyment. The previous verse said transgression is only against your own souls, brief enjoyment, then to Us return and informing. This verse images that brief enjoyment as agriculture: “The life of this world is just like rain We send down from the sky, producing a mixture of plants which humans and animals consume” or “innama mathalu al-hayati al-dunya ka-ma’in anzalnahu mina al-sama’i fa-ikhtalata bihi nabatu al-ardi mimma ya’kulu al-nasu wa al-an‘am.” The rain is the same winds ushering heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit, the same rain that purified at Badr and that made twelve springs gush, now as a similitude for dunya itself. The mixture, or “ikhtalata,” of plants that humans and cattle eat, mirrors the fertile and infertile, the tayyibat for believers to eat without waste, and the hoarded gold to be branded.\n\nThe climax “Then just as the earth looks its best, perfectly beautified, and its people think they have full control over it, there comes to it Our command by night or by day, so We mow it down as if it never flourished yesterday!” or “hatta itha akhathati al-ardu zukhrufaha wa izzayyanat wa zanna ahluha annahum qadiroona alaiha ataha amruna lailan aw naharan fa-ja‘alnaha haseedan ka-an lam taghna bil-ams” uses “zukhruf,” adornment, beautification, the same beautified misdeeds, elegant words that incline hearts, the building on crumbling cliff adorned, the whistling prayer adorned. “Zannoo annahum qadiroona alaiha,” think they have power over it, is the same “We will be forgiven” inheritors and the “Do not march forth, the Fire hotter” clingers, the same “large numbers and arrogance are of no use.” The command, or “amruna,” by night or day, the same overnight while asleep or day while at play that seized societies, the same term that cannot be delayed, mows, or “haseed,” harvested, cut, as if it never flourished, or “ka-an lam taghna,” as if never there, like the wiped as if never lived for Midian and the magicians’ illusions that failed.\n\nThe seal “This is how We make the signs clear for people who reflect” or “kathalika nufassilu al-ayat li-qawmin yatafakkaroon” is the same varying for the thankful, the mindful, the knowledgeable, now for those who reflect, or “yatafakkaroon,” like “Do they not reflect on wonders of heavens and earth and perhaps their end is near, what message after this Quran would they believe in?”\n\nA common error trusts beautified control. The verse shows Our night-or-day command mows it, inviting reflection before brief enjoyment ends and return is informed.",
  "25": "“And Allah invites ˹all˺ to the Home of Peace and guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path” offers the alternative to the mown field that was beautified and controlled. After the parable of rain mixed plants consumed then beautified earth mown as if never flourished, the previous verse ended with “This is how We make the signs clear for people who reflect.” This verse shows what reflection should lead to: “And Allah invites all to the Home of Peace and guides whoever He wills to the Straight Path” or “wa Allahu yad‘oo ila dari al-salam wa yahdi man yashaa’u ila siratin mustaqeem.” The Home of Peace, or “dar al-salam,” is the Gardens under rivers forever, splendid homes, greatest is Allah’s pleasure, ultimate triumph, honourable provision, elevated ranks, mercy close to good-doers, the Home far better for the mindful, the everlasting bliss where prayer is Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise, the opposite of the Fire as home because of what they committed for the heedless pleased with dunya. Peace, or “salam,” is the same salam that greets the Heights, that was not opened for the arrogant, that was prayed as sakeenah at Hunain and in the cave, that is the Most Beautiful Names’ source.\n\nThe invite, or “yad‘oo,” is the same call that gives life, that the Messenger from among yourselves gives, concerned, anxious, gracious, merciful, that the Quran is recited to listen attentively and silently so mercy is shown, that the winds usher mercy, that the five for close relatives, orphans, poor, travellers is, and that the alms for eight as obligation is. Guidance, or “yahdi man yashaa’u,” to the Straight Path, or “siratin mustaqeem,” is the same straight path made clear to the mindful, the perfectly straight path that is your Lord’s Path, the path perfectly straight to follow and not other ways that divide, the same path perfectly straight taking the best of the Tablets, the same path the dog’s panting and the heedless cattle abandon.\n\nThis verse links the mown field’s sudden night-or-day mowing, which took societies at night while asleep, to the Home that is not mown, not beautified then cut, but invites before mowing. The transgressors’ brief enjoyment is the field; the invite is to exchange it, as Allah purchased lives and wealth for Paradise, rejoice, ultimate triumph, whose promise in Torah, Gospel, Quran is truer than any. A common misunderstanding despairs after seeing the field mown as if never there and thinking the Home is unattainable. The verse says the Invite is to all, and guidance is for whom He wills, the same will that elevates with signs but he clung to earth following desires like a dog, the same will that opens chests to Islam for the mindful, the same will that knows goodness to make hear. For those with sickness, it has increased wickedness, but for believers it has increased faith and they rejoice, showing the invite is open, the Straight Path is the same building on taqwa and ridwan, not on the crumbling cliff.",
  "26": "“Those who do good will have the finest reward and ˹even˺ more. Neither gloom nor disgrace will cover their faces. It is they who will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever” defines the reward for those who answer the invite to the Home of Peace. The previous verse offered that invite and guidance to the Straight Path. This verse says “Those who do good will have the finest reward and even more” or “lillatheena ahsanoo al-husna wa ziyadah.” The doers of good, or “allatheena ahsanoo,” are the muhsineen, the good-doers who do ihsan, whose alms are qurbat bringing closer and prayers of Messenger, who will be admitted into mercy, Forgiving, Merciful, who firmly abide and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, who are the group that guides with truth and establishes justice, whose help is Him alone sufficient and whose hearts He united. The finest reward, or “al-husna,” is Paradise, the honourable provision, the great reward, the Gardens forever, the ultimate triumph, the pleasure of Allah, and more, or “ziyadah,” extra, increase, the same increase the foremost taste as Gardens, everlasting Gardens, and the same increase that the best clothing remembrance promises, often understood as the vision of the Lord that the mindful will see, the same vision Moses requested and the mountain could not remain firm for, and that in this world no vision can encompass but He encompasses.\n\nThe protection “Neither gloom nor disgrace will cover their faces” or “wa la yarhaqu wujoohahum qatarun wa la thillah” contrasts with the earlier “No vision can encompass Him but He encompasses” and the earlier “Neither gloom nor disgrace will cover their faces” versus the opposite “Humiliation will cover them as if faces covered with patches of night’s deep darkness” for those who commit evil, whose evil’s equivalent will be humiliation with no protector. Gloom, or “qatar,” dust, and disgrace, or “thillah,” smallness, are the same disgrace that made the harm mosque tumble and the building fuel hypocrisy until hearts are torn, and the apes, and the hoarded treasure’s branding.\n\nThe destiny “It is they who will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-jannati hum fiha khalidoon” mirrors the Fire as home for the heedless and the bearer of burdens, but here it is Paradise’s companions, the same “khalideena fiha abadan” for the Gardens where prayer is Glory, greeting Peace, closing Praise.\n\nA common error thinks good is repaid equally. The verse says finest and even more, the same tenfold and the best reward for what they used to do, where every thirst, hunger, treading, loss, donation, valley is written for best, and where the purchase is lives and wealth for Paradise whose promise in Torah, Gospel, Quran is binding. For the heedless pleased with dunya, the mown field that seemed controlled, the invite to Home of Peace remains the better exchange before the command by night or day mows it as if never flourished.",
  "27": "“As for those who commit evil, the reward of an evil deed is its equivalent. Humiliation will cover them—with no one to protect them from Allah—as if their faces were covered with patches of the night’s deep darkness. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever” gives the counterpart to the finest reward and more for those who do good. The previous verse promised the good-doers Paradise forever without gloom or disgrace covering faces. This verse says “As for those who commit evil, the reward of an evil deed is its equivalent” or “wa allatheena kasaboo al-sayyi’aati jazaa’u sayyi’atin bi-mithliha.” The evil deed, or “sayyi’ah,” is the same sayyi’at that, if committed then repented and believed, finds the Lord All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, but if persisted, is the deed of the rebellious whose example is the dog panting, the hoarders, the sacred-month reallocators, the clingers to land, the mockers, the worst deaf and dumb, whose reward here is equivalent, or “bi-mithliha,” not multiplied as the good’s tenfold, but single, as earlier “Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold, but whoever comes with a bad deed will be punished for only one” and “We will reward the wicked the same.” Equivalence shows the justice, or “qist,” of the weighing just, the same just measure commanded at the city gate with “Hittah” and the just alms for eight.\n\nThe covering “Humiliation will cover them—with no one to protect them from Allah—as if their faces were covered with patches of the night’s deep darkness” or “wa tarhaquhum thillatun ma lahum mina Allahi min aasim ka-annama ughshiyat wujoohuhum qita‘an mina al-laili muzliman” uses “thillah,” disgrace, that covered Pharaoh’s drowning, the apes, the harm mosque’s tumble, the hoarders’ branding, the hypocrites’ neglect, now as darkness patches, the opposite of the radiant sun and reflected moon with measured phases and the alternation signs for the mindful, now night’s deep darkness patches covering faces that earlier would not turn away from idols with no feet, hands, eyes, ears, facing you but not seeing, and that earlier whistled and clapped at the Sacred House.\n\nThe destiny “It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-nari hum fiha khalidoon” mirrors the Gardens’ forever, showing two forever homes built on two foundations: one on taqwa and ridwan, like the mosque founded on righteousness where men love to be purified, the other on the crumbling cliff that tumbles with them into Hell, where the building fuels hypocrisy until hearts are torn, All-Knowing, All-Wise.\n\nA common misunderstanding imagines evil will be overlooked. The verse says its reward is its equivalent, with humiliation as veil, no protector from Allah, unlike the excellent Protector and Helper for those mindful.",
  "28": "“˹Consider˺ the Day We will gather them all together then say to those who associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺, ‘Stay in your places—you and your associate-gods.’ We will separate them from each other, and their associate-gods will say, ‘It was not us that you worshipped!’” sets the resurrection scene for the two forever homes. The previous verse gave the finest reward and more with no gloom and the evil’s equivalent with humiliation like patches of night. This verse says when that recompense is to be given, “Consider the Day We will gather them all together then say to those who associated others with Allah in worship, ‘Stay in your places—you and your associate-gods.’ We will separate them from each other, and their associate-gods will say, ‘It was not us that you worshipped!’” or “wa yawma nahshuruhum jamee‘an thumma naqooloo lillatheena ashrakoo makanakum antum wa shuraka’ukum fa-zayyalna bainahum wa qala shuraka’uhum ma kuntum iyyana ta‘budoon.” The gathering, or “nahshuruhum jamee‘an,” is the same gathering where truth will be informed, where those who took faith as play are forgotten as they forgot the meeting, where the barrier stands and the Heights recognize by marks, where the hypocrites fear a surah will expose, where the Hour, knowledge only with Allah, is too tremendous and will take by surprise. Separation, or “zayyalna bainahum,” is the same separating of evil from good to pile and cast, true losers, and the furqan for the mindful.\n\nThe associate-gods’ denial, “It was not us that you worshipped!” mirrors the devils’ desertion “I see what you do not see, I fear Allah” and the Fire leaders’ “We are free of you,” and the jinn allies’ “We benefited from each other” turned to accusation, showing the helpers who cannot help themselves, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, who if called to guidance cannot hear and facing you cannot see, now speak to disavow.\n\nThis links to the earlier audit “Do they associate those idols which cannot create but are created, which cannot help them or themselves, if you call for guidance they cannot respond, servants like you, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, call and scheme without delay, my Protector is Allah who revealed the Book and protects the righteous.” At gathering, that audit becomes audible from the idols themselves.\n\nA common error expects associate-gods to intercede without permission. The verse shows they separate and deny, just as the earlier “None can intercede except by His permission” taught, so the only intercession that remains is by permission, for the truth-guiding, justice-establishing community, the hopeful on the Heights told “Enter Paradise, no fear,” and the repentant.",
  "29": "“Allah is sufficient as a Witness between each of us that we were totally unaware of your worship” continues the associate-gods’ denial after the separation on the gathering Day. The previous verse had the gods say “It was not us that you worshipped.” This verse adds their oath “Allah is sufficient as a Witness between each of us that we were totally unaware of your worship” or “fa-kafa billahi shaheedan bainana wa bainakum in kunna an ibadatikum la-ghafileen.” The phrase “Allah is sufficient as a Witness between each of us” or “kafa billahi shaheedan bainana wa bainakum” uses the Witness, or “Shaheed,” who is sufficient, like “Allah is sufficient for you and followers” and “Allah is sufficient for me, Lord of the Mighty Throne,” and “He is the Witness between me and you, and the Quran was revealed to warn whoever it reaches.” The associate-gods invoke that Witness to confirm “we were totally unaware of your worship” or “la-ghafileen,” heedless, the same heedless like cattle even less guided, entirely heedless, with hearts not understanding, but here the gods are truthfully unaware, while the worshippers were heedless of signs though they were not unaware in the sense of excuse because “We have certainly brought them a Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy, do they only await fulfilment” and “We did not wrong them but they wronged themselves” and “We gave them stories, messengers with clear proofs but they would not believe.”\n\nThe Witness statement also links to the earlier “We will give them a full account with sure knowledge, for We were never absent” and the “We were never absent” that guarantees the gods’ unawareness is known, and to the “Knower of the seen and unseen will inform you” that will close the dispute about wealth and children, beloveds, and alms. The gods’ heedlessness is innocent, because they were not given faculties to hear, while the worshippers’ heedlessness was culpable, because they were given hearts to understand, eyes to see, ears to hear but did not.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks associate-gods share blame for being worshipped. The verse shows they will testify they were unaware, shifting the ghaflah to the worshipper, as earlier “We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve” and “They neglected Allah so He neglected them” showed guilt lies with the chooser. The next verses, every soul will face what it had done, returned to True Master, fabricated gods fail, and the provider question will expose that Allah is the True Lord, so what is beyond truth except falsehood, how can you be turned away, confirm that the Witness suffices to settle the worship that was not its object but the worshipper’s choice.",
  "30": "“Then and there every soul will face ˹the consequences of˺ what it had done. They all will be returned to Allah—their True Master. And whatever ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them” gives the reckoning after the gods’ witness of unawareness. The previous two verses showed separation and denial, with Allah sufficient as Witness that the gods were unaware of worship. This verse says “Then and there every soul will face the consequences of what it had done” or “hunalika tablu kullu nafsin ma aslafat.” “Tablu,” to be tried, to taste, to face, like the tasting of burning and the tasting of what you hoarded, and the tasting that worldly punishment is yours so taste it and the disbelievers will suffer Fire. “Ma aslafat,” what it sent forward, is the same sending forth that made hands present deeds, that made alms and donations written for best reward, that made thirst, fatigue, hunger, treading, loss, crossing valleys written, and that made hoarded treasure heated to brand.\n\nThe next phrase “They all will be returned to Allah—their True Master. And whatever gods they fabricated will fail them” or “wa ruddoo ila Allahi mawlahumu al-haqqi wa dalla anhum ma kanu yaftaroon” repeats the return to the True Master, or “Mawla al-Haqq,” as earlier “To Him is your return all together, Allah’s promise is always true” and “To Allah all matters will be returned” and “To Him you will be gathered” and “You will be returned to the Knower who will inform you.” The True Master contrasts with the false protectors who cannot help, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, face you but do not see, and who now deny and are unaware, and with parents and siblings taken as allies if they choose disbelief, the true wrongdoers, and with the disbelievers guardians of one another leading to great oppression. Fabricated gods, or “ma kanu yaftaroon,” what they invented, like shares for associates, lowing calf, Sabbath fish, sacred-month reallocating, and sonship claims parroting earlier disbelievers, will stray, or “dalla,” fail, wander, as earlier fabricated gods failed and spending to hinder became regret and large numbers proved of no use.\n\nA common error imagines return can be to another lord. The True Master is the Lord of the Mighty Throne, whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed, whose most beautiful names are to be called upon, whose promise in Torah, Gospel, Quran is true. For the mindful for whom alternation and creation are signs and who do not expect the meeting with displeasure but hope, the return is to the Home of Peace to which He invites and guides whom He wills, the finest reward and more without gloom or disgrace, forever, while for the fabricators the return exposes that the gods they made have failed.",
  "31": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Who provides for you from heaven and earth? Who owns ˹your˺ hearing and sight? Who brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living? And who conducts every affair?’ They will ˹surely˺ say, ‘Allah.’ Say, ‘Will you not then fear ˹Him˺?’” follows the return to the True Master and the failure of fabricated gods with a chain of lordship questions that the polytheists already answer correctly. The phrase “Who provides for you from heaven and earth? Who owns your hearing and sight? Who brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living? And who conducts every affair?” or “man yarzuqukum mina al-sama’i wa al-ardi amman yamliku al-sam‘a wa al-absara wa man yukhriju al-hayya mina al-mayyiti wa yukhriju al-mayyita mina al-hayyi wa man yudabbiru al-amra” lists four lordly acts: provision from heaven and earth, like the twelve-month Record, the winds producing heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit, the twelve springs, the shade, the manna, the alms for eight; ownership of hearing and sight, the same ears they do not hear with and eyes they do not see with and that if seals are placed who can restore except Allah, and that were shown as few in dream and few in eyes; bringing forth living from dead and dead from living, like the Splitter of seeds and dawn breaker and the winds’ heavy clouds raising the dead so perhaps you will be mindful; and conducting every affair, or “yudabbiru al-amra,” the affair-conducting repeated as “creation and command belong to Him, blessed,” whose Throne was established in six Days.\n\nThe expected answer “They will surely say, ‘Allah’” or “fa-sa-yaqooloona Allahu” repeats the earlier “If you ask them who created heavens and earth they will say Allah” and the “To whom belongs what is in heavens and earth? Say to Allah, He has taken mercy upon Himself, He will gather you for the Day no doubt, but those who ruined themselves will never believe.”\n\nThe closing “Say, ‘Will you not then fear Him?’” or “fa-qul a-fa-la tattaqoon” is the same “Will you not then fear Him?” that Hud asked his people after being called fool, and the “Will you not be mindful?” that makes the mindful’s Home far better, the best clothing, the furqan for the mindful. If He provides, owns hearing and sight, brings forth living and dead, and conducts every affair, then fearing, or taqwa, is the fertile response, not associating the helpless who cannot hear guidance, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, facing but not seeing, whose intercession without permission is denied.\n\nA common misunderstanding separates provision from worship. The chain shows worship follows provision and ownership: the Provider and Owner and Bringer and Conductor is the Lord whose kingdom is heavens and earth, He gives life and causes death, no guardian or helper besides Him, so worship is His alone, the ultimate triumph.",
  "32": "“That is Allah—your True Lord. So what is beyond the truth except falsehood? How can you then be turned away?” draws the conclusion from the provision, hearing, sight, life-from-dead, and conducting questions that they answered “Allah.” The phrase “That is Allah—your True Lord. So what is beyond the truth except falsehood? How can you then be turned away?” or “fa-thalikumu Allahu Rabbukumu al-haqqu fa-matha ba‘da al-haqqi illa al-dalalu fa-anna tusrafoon” uses “Rabbukumu al-haqqu,” your True Lord, the Truth, or “al-Haqq,” the Real whose promise is always true, whose Word is perfected, whose purchase is Paradise ultimate triumph, whose noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, whose prior decree made enjoyment lawful. “Fa-matha ba‘da al-haqqi illa al-dalal,” what is beyond truth except misguidance, or “dalal,” the same misguidance that the heedless wander in defiance, that those who even if they see every sign will not believe choose as crooked path, that the dog panting clings to earth following desires, that the cattle even less guided embody. The question “How can you then be turned away?” or “fa-anna tusrafoon” uses “tusrafoon,” to be turned, diverted, as earlier “How can you be deluded?” when Allah brings living from dead and causes seeds to sprout, that is Allah, how can you be turned, and as earlier the reallocated sacred months led far astray, their evil deeds made appealing, Allah does not guide the disbelieving people.\n\nThis verse links the audit of the True Master to whom every soul will be returned and fabricated gods fail, to the creed “No vision can encompass Him but He encompasses all vision, Most Subtle, All-Aware,” and to the invite to the Home of Peace to which He invites and guides whom He wills, the finest reward and more without gloom. If the True Lord is the Provider, Owner, Bringer, Conductor, then beyond Him is only the helpless who cannot create, cannot help themselves or others, cannot hear guidance, servants like you with no feet, hands, eyes, ears, facing but not seeing, whose intercession without permission is void.\n\nA common error imagines a middle path between truth and falsehood. The verse says beyond the True is only misguidance, just as the building on taqwa and ridwan is better or the crumbling cliff tumbles into Fire, and deeds become void for those who deny signs and meeting, and la  yuflihu al-zalimeen, the wicked never succeed. The turn, or “tusrafoon,” is the same turn Allah stands between person and heart to prevent or to let happen, as earlier the hypocrites’ hearts were turned because they did not comprehend, and the mindful are told to remember and see clearly.",
  "33": "“And so your Lord’s decree has been proven true against the rebellious—that they will never believe” seals the turn-away just questioned, showing why those who are turned remain turned. The previous verse asked “That is Allah your True Lord, so what beyond truth except falsehood? How can you be turned away?” This verse says “And so your Lord’s decree has been proven true against the rebellious—that they will never believe” or “kathalika haqqat kalimatu Rabbika ala allatheena fasaqoo annahum la yu’minoon.” The phrase “haqqat kalimatu Rabbika,” the word, decree of your Lord has come true, proved true, the same “kalimah” as the noble Word fulfilled for those who endured, the true promise binding in Torah, Gospel, Quran, whose purchase is lives and wealth for Paradise, and the Word perfected with truth and justice none can change, and He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. The decree against the rebellious, or “fasaqoo,” those who stepped out, is that they will never believe, the same “We will turn away from My signs those who act arrogantly, even if they see every sign will not believe, if they see the right path will not take it but the crooked they will” and “Had Allah known any goodness He would have made them hear, but even if made to hear they would turn heedlessly” and the cattle-like heedless, the dog panting, the worst deaf and dumb.\n\n“Fasaqoo” here are those who did not expect to meet Us, pleased with dunya, heedless of signs, whose home will be Fire because of what they committed, whose likeness is the life like rain mixed plants beautified then the earth adorned and they think they have power over it, then Our command by night or day mows it as if never flourished, for people who reflect. Their rebelliousness is the excess, or “israf,” of the transgressors whose misdeeds have been made appealing, the reallocators, the hoarders, the oath swearers, the mosque of harm builders.\n\nThis verse links the True Lord’s Lordship to the outcome of those who are turned: the decree is not arbitrary but the proven truth of the prior decree that difference after the single community would be settled only by what is true, the same decree that makes wealth and children a test and beloveds a test, and that makes the mindful’s furqan the criterion. A common misunderstanding reads the decree as predestination without cause. The Quran shows cause: rebellious stepping out, denying signs, taking faith as play, hiring devils as allies, feeling secure from planning as losers do, so the decree that they will never believe is the just consequence of the fertile field refusing rain, while the promise remains for those who believe and do good, guided through faith, rivers under feet, prayer Glory, greeting Peace, closing praise.",
  "34": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Can any of your associate-gods originate creation and then resurrect it?’ Say, ‘˹Only˺ Allah originates creation and then resurrects it. How can you then be deluded ˹from the truth˺?’” returns to the creation-resurrection question after the decree that the rebellious will never believe, posing a challenge like the provision, hearing, sight, life-from-dead, conducting chain. The question “Can any of your associate-gods originate creation and then resurrect it?” or “qul hal min shuraka’ikum man yabda’u al-khalqa thumma yu‘eeduhu” uses “yabda’u,” to originate, to begin, as earlier “He originates the creation then resurrects it to reward justly” and “We brought you into being, you will be brought to life again” as uprightness, and the staff’s snake and the hand’s whiteness, the earth’s springs, the winds’ heavy clouds producing every fruit similarly raising the dead, all origination. “Yu‘eeduhu,” to repeat, to resurrect, is the return all together to the Knower who will inform you, the gathering where truth will be informed and fabricated gods fail, the Home of Peace invite versus the Fire home for the heedless.\n\nThe answer “Say, ‘Only Allah originates creation and then resurrects it. How can you then be deluded from the truth?’” or “qul Allahu yabda’u al-khalqa thumma yu‘eeduhu fa-anna tu’fakoon” uses “tu’fakoon,” to be deluded, turned, as earlier “How can you then be deluded” when that is Allah your True Lord and “How can you be deluded when He brings living from dead” and the reallocated sacred months led far astray, their evil deeds made appealing. Delusion here is like the hypocrites’ “Are you awaiting except one of the two best things, we await affliction” and the hoarders’ “We will be forgiven” delusion, the dog’s panting delusion that clinging to earth elevates.\n\nThis challenge mirrors the earlier “Who provides from heaven and earth? They will say Allah, will you not then fear?” and “Can any of your associate-gods guide to the truth? Only Allah guides, who is more worthy to be followed,” showing creation-resurrection is the Lordship proof before guidance proof, both belonging to the Lord whose kingdom is heavens and earth, He gives life and causes death, no guardian or helper besides Him.\n\nA common error imagines origination without resurrection suffices. The verse joins them: the One who began the single soul, made spouse for tranquility, light burden to heavy, prayed for good offspring, can repeat, so turning from resurrection to brief enjoyment like rain mixed plants beautified then mown is delusion. For the mindful for whom alternation and creation are signs, origination-resurrection is the sign that should make the heedless expect the meeting, become mindful, and be with the truthful, whose lives and wealth are purchased for Paradise, ultimate triumph.",
  "35": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, ‘Can any of your associate-gods guide to the truth?’ Say, ‘˹Only˺ Allah guides to the truth.’ Who then is more worthy to be followed: the One Who guides to the truth or those who cannot find the way unless guided? What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” pairs guidance with origination, completing the lordship audit. The previous verse asked can any of your associates originate and resurrect, answer only Allah, how can you be deluded? This verse asks “Can any of your associate-gods guide to the truth?” or “qul hal min shuraka’ikum man yahdi ila al-haqqi.” The question uses “yahdi ila al-haqqi,” to guide to the truth, the same huda that is the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy, insights, the straight path made clear to the mindful, the Tablets’ fundamentals, the light with which the unlettered Prophet relieves burdens, the invitation to the Home of Peace to which He invites and guides whom He wills, the finest reward and more for the good-doers. The associate-gods’ inability to guide mirrors their inability to create, help, hear, see, have feet, hands, eyes, ears, face you but not see, call to guidance but not hear, servants like you, whose intercession without permission is denied and whose worship they were unaware of and whose denial was witnessed by Allah sufficient.\n\nThe answer “Say, ‘Only Allah guides to the truth.’ Who then is more worthy to be followed: the One Who guides to the truth or those who cannot find the way unless guided?” or “qul Allahu yahdi lil-haqqi a-fa-man yahdi ila al-haqqi ahaqqu an yuttaba‘a amman la yahdi illa an yuhda” contrasts the Guide, or “yahdi,” with the guided, the same contrast as “Who provides? Who owns hearing and sight? Who brings living from dead? Who conducts?” They will say Allah. The associate-gods are the “la yahdi illa an yuhda,” cannot guide unless guided, like the cattle heedless, the dog panting, the building on crumbling cliff needing support, the worst deaf and dumb who do not understand. Worthiness to be followed, or “ahaqqu an yuttaba‘a,” is the same following that defines the Ear of good, the unlettered Prophet believed in and followed to be rightly guided, the following that the rich were asked to believe and struggle with the Messenger, the following that the foremost did in goodness and were pleased and prepared Gardens, ultimate triumph.\n\nThe challenge “What is the matter with you? How do you judge?” or “fa-ma lakum kaifa tahkumoon” is the same “How do you judge” after “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies or deny revelations, the wicked will never succeed” and after the provision, hearing, sight audit. It mirrors the earlier “Evil is what they have done” for choosing small price and “Evil indeed is what they have done” for hindering.\n\nA common misunderstanding follows what seems popular, like the rich asking to be left with the helpless, the hypocrites’ oath to please, the hoarders’ heated gold. The Quran asks who is more worthy to be followed: the Guide to truth is ahaqqu, more right, than the guided who cannot guide unless guided. For the mindful granted furqan, the answer is to follow the Guide to truth, whose creation and command belong to Him, blessed, whose most beautiful names are to be called upon.",
  "36": "“Most of them follow nothing but ˹inherited˺ assumptions. ˹And˺ surely assumptions can in no way replace the truth. Allah is indeed All-Knowing of what they do” diagnoses the epistemology behind preferring the guided who cannot guide unless guided and the delusion from truth. The previous verse asked who is more worthy to be followed: the One who guides to truth or those who cannot find the way unless guided, what is the matter how do you judge? This verse answers what they follow instead of the Guide: “Most of them follow nothing but inherited assumptions” or “wa ma yattabi‘u aktharuhum illa zanna.” The phrase “inherited assumptions,” or “zann,” conjecture, supposition, is the same assumption for which “If you were to obey most of those on earth, they would lead you away, they follow nothing but assumptions and do nothing but lie” and “Surely assumptions can in no way replace the truth” and “Had it not been for a prior decree” and the “most of them did not know” and “most of them are rebellious” and the worst deaf and dumb. The “most of them” shows the problem is majoritarian, like the large numbers and arrogance that are of no use, the spending to hinder that becomes regret, the sacred-month reallocation that increases in disbelief by beautified deeds.\n\nThe principle “And surely assumptions can in no way replace the truth” or “inna al-zanna la yughni mina al-haqqi shay’an” uses “la yughni,” to avail, to suffice, as earlier “Your forces no matter how numerous will not avail” and “Their wealth and children will not avail” and the treasure heated will not avail, the same “la yughni” that makes the promise to give better than what was taken only if goodness is found. Assumptions avail nothing against the truth, or “al-haqq,” the same Truth beyond which is only misguidance, as earlier “That is Allah your True Lord, so what beyond truth except falsehood, how can you be turned away” and the true promise binding in Torah, Gospel, Quran.\n\nThe seal “Allah is indeed All-Knowing of what they do” or “inna Allaha aleemun bima yaf‘aloon” is the knowing that makes the weighing just, that wrote the twelve-month Record, that knows sirr and najwa and unseen, that stands between person and heart, that knows goodness to make hear, that hears the weeping for no mounts, that knows the hoard, that knows the reallocation, that will inform you of what you used to do when returned to the Knower.\n\nA common error treats inherited assumption as tradition to respect. The Quran calls it zann that cannot replace haqq, the same zann that made the Sabbath town’s fish abundant on forbidden day seem like a trick and the gate changers’ word change seem minor. For the community that inherited Scripture well-versed yet seized lowly gain, the remedy is to follow the Guide to truth, hold firmly to the Scripture where reward is never discounted, listen attentively and silently so mercy is shown, remember inwardly morning and evening without heedlessness, and reflect on wonders and perhaps end is near.",
  "37": "The claim that this book could never have been \"produced by anyone other than Allah\" answers directly the problem raised just before it, where most people were said to follow nothing but assumptions. Assumption was the disease; revelation is the cure. Guesswork cannot reach certainty about God, the unseen, or the purpose of life, so the only reliable path is a message that actually comes from the One who knows. The verse therefore turns from criticising human guessing to presenting the alternative: a Quran whose source is not human at all.\n\nThe wording is a denial of possibility, not merely a denial of fact. It is not being said that the Quran happens not to be invented; it is being said that inventing it lies outside human reach. The Arabic behind \"produced\" here is \"yuftara,\" which comes from \"iftira,\" meaning to fabricate or to make something up and falsely attach it to someone else. Fabrication always leaves traces, contradictions, self-interest, and the fingerprints of its author. The Quran offers itself for that kind of inspection, and elsewhere it invites the test openly: \"If it had been from anyone other than Allah, they would have certainly found in it many inconsistencies.\"\n\nTwo reasons are then given. First, \"it is a confirmation of what came before.\" The Quran does not arrive as a stranger to earlier revelation; it agrees with the core message brought by Moses, Jesus, and the prophets before them, calling to one God, accountability, and moral responsibility. An Arab merchant living in Mecca had no library of scriptures to copy from, yet the message matches the deepest content of what earlier prophets taught while correcting what people later distorted.\n\nSecond, it is \"an explanation of the Scripture.\" The word behind this, \"tafsil,\" means to lay something out in detail, part by part. Where earlier revelation was general or had become unclear, this Book separates truth from later additions and spells out belief, worship, law, and character clearly.\n\nThe conclusion, \"from the Lord of all worlds,\" matches the claim to its source: a message for all peoples must come from the Lord of all peoples, not from one tribe's imagination.",
  "38": "\"Or do they claim, \"He made it up!\"?\" repeats the accusation that the previous verse had already ruled out, and this repetition is deliberate. Having stated that fabricating such a book is beyond human power, the Quran now moves from statement to test. Anyone can call something a forgery; the honest way to prove it is to reproduce it. So the reply is not an argument about feelings but an open challenge: \"Produce one sûrah like it then, and seek help from whoever you can— other than Allah—if what you say is true!\"\n\nNotice how small the demand is. Not a whole book, not ten chapters, but a single \"sûrah,\" and the shortest chapters of the Quran are only a few lines. The challenger is also allowed unlimited assistance, \"seek help from whoever you can,\" meaning poets, orators, scholars, and even the false gods they prayed to. The only excluded helper is Allah Himself, since the point in dispute is whether Allah is the source.\n\nThe people addressed were not casual critics. Pre-Islamic Arabia treated language as its highest art. Poets were the pride of their tribes, competitions of eloquence were public events, and a gifted speaker could shape a tribe's reputation. If any audience in history was equipped to answer such a challenge, it was this one. Their leaders had every motive to do so, since producing a matching chapter would have ended the movement in an afternoon and saved them years of boycott, persecution, and eventually war. They chose fighting over composing.\n\nThe same challenge appears at different levels in the Quran, which shows how confident it is. In Surah al-Baqarah the demand is put as, \"then produce a sûrah like it and call your helpers other than Allah, if what you say is true,\" with the added prediction, \"and you will never be able to do so.\" In Surah Hud the offer is enlarged: \"Produce ten fabricated sûrahs like it.\" And in Surah al-Isra it is widened to the whole of humanity and jinn together, who \"could not produce its equal, no matter how they supported each other.\"\n\nThe challenge still stands, unanswered.",
  "39": "\"In fact, they ˹hastily˺ rejected the Book without comprehending it\" exposes what really lay behind the accusation of forgery mentioned a moment earlier. Their problem was not that they had studied the Quran carefully and found it wanting. They rejected it first and looked for reasons afterwards. The verse describes a refusal that comes before understanding, which is the opposite of how a fair person deals with a serious claim.\n\nThere is a second layer in \"and before the fulfilment of its warnings.\" The Arabic word here is \"ta'wil,\" which in this context means the final outcome of something, the point at which a promise or a warning actually happens. The Quran spoke of a coming judgment, of nations being humbled, of truth prevailing. Those outcomes had not yet unfolded when the deniers passed their verdict. So they condemned a book whose content they had not grasped and whose predicted results they had not yet waited to see. It is like a person tearing up a doctor's prescription without reading it and without giving the medicine any time to work.\n\nThis is not a new pattern. \"Similarly, those before them were in denial\" places the Meccan leaders in a long line: the people of Noah who mocked the ark, the people of Hud and Salih who dismissed their prophets as ordinary men, Pharaoh who called Moses a magician. In every case the rejection came quickly and confidently, and in every case the outcome arrived later and settled the argument.\n\nThat is why the verse ends with an invitation to look, not merely to listen: \"See then what was the end of the wrongdoers!\" The ruins of earlier peoples lay along the trade routes the Meccans travelled each year. The Quran repeatedly asks them to treat history as evidence rather than scenery.\n\nA useful point for any reader is the standard being set here. Faith is not being demanded blindly; hasty rejection is being criticised precisely because it is blind. The Quran asks to be understood before it is judged, and it asks its warnings to be weighed rather than laughed at.",
  "40": "\"Some of them will ˹eventually˺ believe in it; others will not\" divides the deniers just described into two futures. The previous verse showed a community rejecting the Book without understanding it, and it would be easy to assume that such people are all finished. This verse refuses that conclusion. Within the same hostile crowd there are hearts that will still open.\n\nMeccan history proved it. Among those who once opposed the Prophet fiercely were men who later became pillars of the community: Umar ibn al-Khattab, who set out angry and returned a believer; Khalid ibn al-Walid, who commanded armies against the Muslims before fighting alongside them; Amr ibn al-As; Abu Sufyan and many of the Quraysh who accepted Islam at the conquest of Mecca. Others, like Abu Jahl and Abu Lahab, died holding their rejection to the end. The verse quietly prepares the Prophet for both outcomes so that neither surprises him.\n\nThere is a strong practical implication here for anyone who calls others to good. Since it cannot be known in advance which category a person belongs to, no one should be written off. The Prophet kept speaking gently to people who insulted him, and history repeatedly rewarded that patience. Treating an opponent as permanently lost is a judgement no human being has the knowledge to make.\n\nThe closing words, \"And your Lord knows best the corruptors,\" draw the line between what people can see and what only God can see. The word \"mufsidun,\" translated here as \"the corruptors,\" refers to those who spread ruin, whether by breaking trust, spreading falsehood, or attacking what is good. Their outward behaviour may look identical to that of someone who is merely confused or badly informed. Only Allah knows which stubbornness comes from a heart set on corruption and which comes from ignorance that can still be healed.\n\nSo the verse gives the believer two instructions at once: keep inviting, because change is genuinely possible, and leave the final verdict on hearts to the One who knows them. Certainty about a person's ultimate state belongs to Allah alone, not to those who observe from outside.",
  "41": "\"If they deny you, then say, \"My deeds are mine and your deeds are yours\"\" gives the Prophet a response for the moment when argument has clearly run its course. The point is not that evidence had failed, since the challenge to produce a chapter had just been made and left unanswered. The point is that when someone has decided not to accept, continuing to quarrel serves nothing. The instruction is to state the parting of ways calmly and move on.\n\nThe wording separates responsibility rather than expressing anger. Each side owns its own record. This matches the Quran's constant principle that no soul carries the burden of another. It is worth noticing what the verse does not say. It does not say the two paths are equally valid, nor that truth and falsehood are simply matters of taste. It says that each party will answer for its own choice, which is a statement about accountability, not a statement that everything is acceptable.\n\n\"You are free of what I do and I am free of what you do!\" makes the separation complete and mutual. The Arabic idea behind \"free of\" is \"bara'ah,\" meaning to be clear of something, to have no share in it and no liability for it. A believer is not implicated in the idol worship of the deniers, and they are not implicated in the worship the Prophet offers. The relationship of shared religious life is cut, even while ordinary human dealings continue.\n\nA closely related passage is Surah al-Kafirun, which ends with the famous words that give each side its own religion. The two passages agree in substance, though the tone differs slightly: al-Kafirun refuses a proposed compromise in worship, while this verse closes an argument that has become pointless.\n\nThis has a real application. Believers often exhaust themselves trying to win every debate with someone determined not to be convinced. The Quran teaches that the message must be delivered clearly and honestly, but hearts are not conquered by pressure. After the truth is presented, dignity, distance, and trust in Allah are wiser than endless dispute.",
  "42": "\"Some of them listen to what you say\" describes people who were physically present at the recitation. They came to the gatherings, they heard every word, and some even listened in secret at night, as reports about the Meccan leaders indicate. Yet the verse immediately shows that this kind of listening changes nothing: \"but can you make the deaf hear even though they do not understand?\"\n\nThe question is directed to the Prophet as a comfort and as a limit. He is being told that the failure is not his. His voice reached their ears; what he could not do is force meaning into a heart that refuses it. The Arabic word for this hearing, \"sam',\" in Quranic usage often carries the sense of listening in order to accept, not just receiving sound. That is why the same book can say that people heard and also that they are deaf. Their organs worked; their attention did not.\n\nThe added phrase \"even though they do not understand\" identifies the real blockage. If deafness were the only problem, a louder voice or a written copy might help. But the verse says they do not use their reason, so no delivery method would succeed. A modern comparison is easy to picture: a student sitting in the front row of a lecture with the teacher's words entering his ears while his mind is entirely elsewhere. At the end he can honestly say he heard nothing useful, though every word was audible.\n\nElsewhere the Quran states the same principle in a striking way, describing those who \"have hearts they do not understand with, and eyes they do not see with, and ears they do not hear with,\" and it applies the description to human beings who have surrendered their faculties.\n\nThe lesson for a reader of the Quran is uncomfortable but valuable. Merely hearing recitation, even beautifully performed, is not the goal. Listening in the Quranic sense means attending with the mind engaged and the heart willing to act. Without that, a person may spend a lifetime near the words and remain untouched by them.",
  "43": "\"And some of them look at you\" moves from the ear to the eye, completing the picture begun in the previous verse. There were people who watched the Prophet closely every day: his honesty in trade, his care for the poor, his patience under insult, the transformation in those who followed him. His life was itself an argument. Yet the verse asks, \"but can you guide the blind even though they cannot see?\"\n\nSeeing here means recognising what is in front of you. The Meccans had known Muhammad since childhood and had nicknamed him the trustworthy one before he announced his message. Nothing in his character supported the accusation that he was a liar or a madman. To watch such a man and conclude that he was a fraud is a failure of sight, not a lack of evidence. The Quran makes this explicit in another place, saying that it is not the eyes that turn blind, but rather the hearts within the chests.\n\nPairing hearing and sight is deliberate, because these are the two doors through which a person receives the signs of God. The Quran often mentions them together when describing both guidance and its refusal. When both doors are shut from the inside, no messenger can open them, and this is exactly why the Prophet is repeatedly reminded that his duty is to convey, not to compel.\n\nThere is also a mercy in the question for the one who carries the message. A sincere teacher, parent, or friend often blames himself when the person he loves refuses the truth. These two verses take that weight off the Prophet's shoulders. He was not lacking clarity or kindness; the fault lay in a refusal that no amount of skill can override.\n\nFor anyone thinking about their own state, the verses raise a fair question. Signs of God are everywhere, in creation and in scripture. Whether a person notices them depends less on the strength of the eyes than on the honesty of the heart behind them. Asking Allah for that inner sight is part of what guidance means.",
  "44": "\"Indeed, Allah does not wrong people in the least\" answers a doubt that naturally arises after two verses describing people who cannot hear or see the truth. Someone might ask: if they were left in that state, is that not unfair? The reply is direct. God does no injustice to anyone, not even by the smallest amount. Their deafness and blindness were self-inflicted, the result of repeated refusal until refusing became their nature.\n\nThe word behind \"wrong\" is \"zulm,\" which in Arabic means putting something where it does not belong, and it covers oppression, injustice, and taking what is not owed. Denying it of God is not merely saying that He chooses to be fair. Justice is part of who He is. A well-known sacred hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim expresses this beautifully, where Allah says that He has forbidden injustice for Himself and made it forbidden among people, so they should not wrong one another.\n\n\"But it is people who wrong themselves\" places the responsibility exactly where it belongs. This is one of the Quran's central moral statements. When a person turns away from guidance, the loss is entirely his own; God gains nothing from obedience and loses nothing from rebellion. The image is of someone who owns a lamp and refuses to light it, then complains about the darkness. The lamp was given; the choice was his.\n\nIt is worth clearing up a misunderstanding here. Some read verses about God sealing hearts and conclude that people are punished for a state forced upon them. Verses like this one rule that out. The sealing described elsewhere in the Quran is the settled outcome of a person's own long insistence, not an arbitrary act against an innocent. The Quran keeps both truths together: Allah's will governs everything, and human beings genuinely choose and are genuinely responsible.\n\nPractically, this reframes how a believer views his own failures. Rather than blaming circumstances, family, or fate, he is taught to look first at his own choices, because that is the only place where repair can begin.",
  "45": "\"On the Day He will gather them, it will be as if they had not stayed ˹in the world˺ except for an hour of a day\" shifts the scene from earthly argument to the moment when all argument ends. The people who demanded to know when judgment would come, and who lived as though this life were everything, will find that the entire span of their existence has shrunk in memory to something like a single hour.\n\nThis shrinking of time is a recurring theme. In Surah al-Mu'minun, when the dwellers of the Fire are asked how many years they stayed on earth, they answer that they stayed a day or part of a day, and they are told they stayed only a little. The comparison is not a trick; it is a matter of proportion. A life of seventy years placed beside an unending existence genuinely is a moment, in the same way that a long holiday feels like nothing once it is over and normal life resumes.\n\nThe added phrase, \"˹as though they were only˺ getting to know one another,\" captures how brief and slight worldly relationships will appear. People spend their lives forming attachments, alliances, and rivalries. On that Day it will feel as if they had barely been introduced before being separated and called to account. Anyone who has attended a reunion decades later knows how quickly years collapse into a short conversation.\n\nThen comes the verdict: \"Lost indeed will be those who denied the meeting with Allah, and were not ˹rightly˺ guided!\" The loss is defined by one thing above all, denying \"the meeting with Allah.\" Belief in this meeting is what gives every action weight. Remove it, and a person invests everything in an hour and keeps nothing for what comes after.\n\nThe verse also links denial with lack of guidance, showing that the two go together. Rejecting the meeting is not a harmless opinion; it steers a whole life in the wrong direction. Keeping that meeting in view, on the other hand, quietly reorders a person's priorities without needing any further sermon.",
  "46": "\"Whether We show you ˹O Prophet˺ some of what We threaten them with, or cause you to die ˹before that˺\" speaks to the Prophet about a question that must have pressed on him during the hardest Meccan years. He had warned his people of consequences, and they mocked him because nothing seemed to happen. Would he live to see the outcome, or would he be taken before it? The verse says both possibilities are open, and neither changes the result.\n\nNotice the word \"some.\" Even the punishments seen in this world are only a portion of what awaits. The Prophet did in fact witness part of it. Within a few years of migrating to Medina he saw the leaders who had tortured his companions killed at Badr, and he later entered Mecca peacefully as the city that expelled him opened its gates. Yet the greater reckoning remained beyond this life.\n\n\"To Us is their return\" is the heart of the verse. Human justice depends on living long enough to see it done. Divine justice does not. A tyrant who dies comfortably in bed has not escaped anything; he has simply arrived early at the court that matters. This reframes how believers understand delay. Delay is not denial, and it is not weakness on God's part.\n\nThe closing words, \"and Allah is a Witness over what they do,\" add something more searching than a legal record. A witness sees the act as it happens, including the intention behind it and the details no one else noticed. Later in this same surah the point is made in even wider terms, that there is no activity anyone is engaged in \"except that We are a Witness over you while doing it,\" and that not an atom's weight is hidden from the Lord.\n\nThere is comfort here for anyone working for a good cause and not seeing results. The one who conveys the message is responsible for conveying it; the outcome belongs to Allah, whether it appears in a person's lifetime or long after he is gone.",
  "47": "\"And for every community there is a messenger\" states a principle of divine justice that runs through the whole Quran. No nation has been judged without first being warned. God does not hold people accountable for guidance they never received. Surah Fatir puts it plainly: \"There is no community that has not had a warner.\" Muslims believe many such messengers existed whose names were never recorded in scripture, since the Quran itself says that some messengers were mentioned to the Prophet and others were not.\n\nThe Arabic word \"ummah,\" translated here as \"community,\" refers to a people joined by a shared time, place, or way of life. The verse is describing how God has dealt with humanity throughout history: a messenger comes, the message is made clear, and only then does the verdict follow.\n\n\"After their messenger has come, judgment is passed on them in all fairness\" describes what happens next. The Arabic phrase behind \"in all fairness\" is \"bi al-qist,\" meaning strict, balanced equity, giving each person exactly what is due. Two things are weighed: what the messenger delivered and how the people responded. This applies both to the punishments that struck earlier nations in this world and to the final judgment.\n\nThe closing assurance, \"and they are not wronged,\" rules out any possibility of unfair treatment. No one will be punished for a message that never reached him, and no one will be given a heavier reckoning than his actual conduct deserves.\n\nThere is an important implication for how believers think about people who have never genuinely heard the message. Reliable scholars have discussed such cases carefully, and many held that those who never received a true call are dealt with by Allah's justice and mercy, not condemned for what was hidden from them. The Quran's own words, that no soul is burdened beyond its capacity and that God does not punish until He sends a messenger, support this caution.\n\nFor those who have received the message, the same principle carries a weight of responsibility. Knowledge that has reached a person becomes part of the record that will be examined.",
  "48": "\"They ask ˹the believers˺, \"When will this threat come to pass if what you say is true?\"\" records a question that sounds like curiosity but functions as mockery. Having heard the previous verse's statement that every community faces judgment after its messenger arrives, the Meccan leaders responded by demanding a date. In their minds, a warning with no timetable was proof that the warning was empty.\n\nThe Arabic word for the warning here relates to a promise or an appointed threat, and the challenge \"if what you say is true\" is the same taunt they used elsewhere. It was not a request for information. Had a date been given, the likely response would have been to laugh and wait, and the Quran shows no interest in satisfying that kind of demand. Similar mocking demands appear in other passages, such as those who asked for stones to rain down from the sky if the message were really from God, and those who asked scornfully when the resurrection would occur.\n\nThe mistake behind the question is easy to name. They treated the absence of immediate punishment as evidence that no punishment exists. That reasoning fails even in daily life. A patient who ignores a doctor's warning and feels fine for years has not disproved the diagnosis; he has only used up time. A debt does not disappear because the due date has not yet arrived.\n\nThere is also a hidden assumption that God's ways must match human impatience. People who hold power tend to strike immediately, because delay risks losing the chance. Divine power has no such limitation, so delay costs nothing and may serve mercy, giving room for some to change, as the earlier verse noted that some of them would eventually believe.\n\nThe verses that follow answer this demand step by step: first by pointing out that even the Prophet does not control his own benefit or harm, then by explaining that every community has a fixed term, and finally by asking whether they truly understand what they are so eager to hurry.",
  "49": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah\"\" is the first answer to their demand for a date. Before explaining anything about timing, the Prophet is told to correct their picture of who he is. They spoke as though he were the one holding the punishment back or releasing it. He replies that he does not even control his own good and harm, let alone the fate of nations.\n\nThis is one of the clearest statements in the Quran against exaggerating the status of prophets. Muhammad was the most honoured of human beings, yet he is instructed to announce his own dependence on God. The same point is made in Surah al-A'raf, where he is told to say that had he known the unseen, he would have secured much good for himself and no harm would have touched him. Communities in history have slipped into treating their prophets and saints as independent sources of benefit and harm; this verse closes that door before it opens.\n\nThen the real answer comes: \"For each community there is an appointed term.\" The Arabic word \"ajal\" means a fixed, appointed time set in advance. Nations, like individuals, have a lifespan known to Allah. Their prosperity is not endless and their punishment is not random; both fall within a schedule already written.\n\n\"When their time arrives, they cannot delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it\" underlines how exact that schedule is. The same wording appears in Surah al-A'raf, \"For each community there is an appointed term. When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it,\" and in Surah an-Nahl, where it follows the statement that if Allah punished people immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single living being on earth.\n\nThat connection explains the delay the Meccans were mocking. It was not weakness or absence. It was deliberate patience within a fixed term, and mocking such patience is a poor use of the time it grants.",
  "50": "\"Tell them ˹O Prophet˺, \"Imagine if His torment were to overcome you by night or day\"\" invites the mockers to actually picture the thing they keep demanding. They had asked when the threatened punishment would arrive. Instead of naming a date, the verse asks them to sit with the reality for a moment, because most people who joke about danger have never seriously imagined it.\n\nMentioning both \"by night or day\" removes every escape. Punishment that comes at night finds people asleep and defenceless; punishment that comes by day finds them busy in markets and fields, equally unprepared. The Quran describes earlier nations struck in exactly these ways, some by a cry at daybreak and some in the middle of their ordinary business. There is no safe hour.\n\nThe word translated \"overcome,\" in Arabic close to the sense of coming upon someone suddenly and unexpectedly, matters here. What they were demanding was not something they could brace for. Anyone who has lived through an earthquake or a sudden accident knows how quickly confidence collapses when the ground moves without warning.\n\nThe verse then turns their demand into a question about their own understanding: \"do the wicked realize what they are ˹really˺ asking Him to hasten?\" The word \"mujrimun,\" rendered here as \"the wicked,\" refers to those who cut themselves off from what is right through deliberate crime and sin. The question implies that they do not realise it at all. They were treating divine punishment as a debating point, something to score against the Prophet, without grasping that they were asking for their own destruction to arrive sooner.\n\nEveryday life offers small versions of this. A person may laugh at a warning about his health while the danger is invisible, and lose all humour the moment symptoms appear. The Quran repeatedly tries to move people from that late seriousness to an earlier one, because seriousness that arrives after the event is useless.\n\nThe next verse presses exactly that point, asking whether belief will only come once the punishment is already upon them.",
  "51": "\"Will you believe in it only after it has overtaken you?\" exposes the emptiness of faith that arrives too late. It follows naturally from the challenge in the previous verse, where the wicked were asked whether they understood what they were demanding. Now they are asked about the moment of arrival. Yes, they will believe then, but that belief will be worth nothing, because it is no longer a choice made from trust in the unseen. It is simply seeing.\n\nThe single word \"Now?\" carries enormous weight. It is a rebuke placed at the exact instant of realisation, and its force can be felt if one imagines a student asking for extra time after the exam papers have been collected. This same word appears later in this very surah at one of its most dramatic moments. When Pharaoh was drowning and finally announced that he believed in the God of Israel, the answer came, \"Now? But you always disobeyed and were one of the corruptors.\" The pattern is identical, and placing the two passages side by side shows that this is a fixed rule, not a one-off scene.\n\n\"But you always wanted to hasten it!\" completes the irony. They had spent their days demanding the punishment as proof, treating it as a joke. When it comes, they want it delayed and they want their faith accepted. The very thing they mocked becomes the thing they fear.\n\nThe principle behind this is important and is stated elsewhere in the Quran and in the Sunnah: repentance is accepted while the door remains open, and the door closes when death or the promised punishment actually appears. A well-known hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi says that Allah accepts the repentance of His servant as long as the death rattle has not reached his throat. Faith has value because it is offered before proof becomes overwhelming.\n\nThat is why the Quran keeps urging people to respond now, in the ordinary quiet of an ordinary day, when belief still costs something and therefore still means something.",
  "52": "\"Then the wrongdoers will be told, \"Taste the torment of eternity!\"\" moves the scene forward from the moment of arrival to the sentence itself. The command to \"taste\" is used often in the Quran for punishment, and it is chosen deliberately. Tasting is direct, personal experience. These were people who treated the warning as an abstract idea worth arguing about; now the argument becomes something felt on the tongue, no longer a debate but a condition.\n\n\"The torment of eternity\" answers their earlier impatience in a terrible way. They had wanted the punishment hurried; what they receive has no end. The Arabic word \"khuld\" means lasting permanence, remaining without cessation. This is the Quran's consistent description of the final state of those who die rejecting the truth, and it stands in exact contrast to the \"hour of a day\" that their whole worldly life will seem to have lasted. A moment of gain purchased at the price of endless loss is the worst trade a person can make.\n\nThen comes the question that removes any complaint: \"Are you not rewarded except for what you used to commit?\" Nothing extra is being added to their account. The punishment is described as the recompense for their own earning, using a word that means acquiring or committing deeds by one's own effort. This connects back to the earlier statement that Allah does not wrong people in the least, but people wrong themselves.\n\nThe wording \"what you used to commit\" also points to habit rather than a single slip. It describes a settled pattern of conduct maintained over a lifetime. The Quran nowhere presents God as searching for reasons to punish; it presents a person's own repeated, knowing choices being handed back to him.\n\nA misunderstanding worth clearing here is the idea that Hell is a disproportionate response to a short life. The Quran's answer, seen across many passages, is that what is punished is not the length of the sin but its nature: a permanent refusal of the Creator, held to the last breath, and only interrupted by death, not by any change of heart.",
  "53": "\"They ask you ˹O Prophet˺, \"Is this true?\"\" records a different tone from the mocking demand a few verses earlier. Here the question sounds like the doubt of people who half suspect the answer and want to hear it confirmed. Whether it came from genuine uncertainty or from lingering sarcasm, the reply is the same, and it is remarkably firm.\n\n\"Say, \"Yes, by my Lord! Most certainly it is true!\"\" The Prophet is instructed to swear an oath. This is one of only three places in the Quran where the Messenger is commanded to take an oath by his Lord, and all three concern the reality of resurrection and judgment: this verse, a passage in Surah Saba where the deniers claim the Hour will never come to them, and a passage in Surah at-Taghabun where they claim they will never be raised. The repetition of this exact device around one subject shows how central it is. Everything the Quran teaches about morality depends on there being a day when accounts are settled.\n\nSwearing \"by my Lord\" rather than by anything created is itself instructive. Islam permits oaths only by Allah, and a well-known hadith recorded in the collections of Bukhari and Muslim states that whoever swears must swear by Allah or remain silent. The Prophet's oath here is not emotional insistence; it is the strongest possible form of testimony given by a man whose honesty his own enemies never doubted before he began to preach.\n\n\"And you will have no escape\" closes the door on the alternative they were hoping for. The word behind \"escape\" carries the sense of frustrating a pursuer, slipping away, rendering someone unable to catch you. No one can outrun this appointment. Death takes people from every place and every level of power, and none of them negotiates a delay.\n\nThe answer is short because the question does not need a long reply. It needs certainty, delivered plainly by someone who knows, and then the space for the listener to sit with it.",
  "54": "\"If every wrongdoer were to possess everything in the world, they would surely ransom themselves with it\" measures the value of that Day against the value of everything people spend their lives collecting. The picture is deliberately extreme. Not a fortune, but the entire earth, owned by a single person, and offered instantly as a payment to escape. And the offer would be refused.\n\nThe wording connects to a scene in Surah Ali Imran, where it is said that even an earth full of gold would not be accepted from those who die in disbelief. The reason is not that God needs or refuses wealth. It is that wealth was never the currency of that court. What is weighed there is faith and deeds, and no amount of anything else can be exchanged for them. Money, which solves almost every problem in this life, becomes completely useless in the one situation that matters most.\n\n\"They will hide ˹their˺ remorse when they see the torment\" describes something psychologically true. The Arabic word \"nadamah\" means regret over what one has done, the sinking recognition that a chance existed and was wasted. Interpreters have explained the hiding of it in more than one way: some said they conceal their regret out of shame before those they once led or mocked, while others said the shock is so overwhelming that the regret is swallowed and cannot even be expressed. Either way, this is regret that arrives when it can no longer produce any change, which is the most painful kind.\n\n\"And they will be judged in all fairness, and none will be wronged\" ends the verse the way divine justice is always described in this passage. The judgment is by \"qist,\" exact equity. No one receives a heavier sentence than his conduct earned, and no one is punished for another's crime.\n\nThere is a quiet warning here for anyone who assumes that resources, influence, or connections will always find a way out. Those tools work only inside a system that accepts them. This verse describes the one setting where they buy nothing at all.",
  "55": "\"Surely to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth\" supplies the reason behind everything the previous verses claimed. If the wrongdoer's imagined ransom of the whole earth would be worthless, it is because the earth was never his to offer. Ownership belongs entirely to Allah. A servant cannot buy his freedom with his master's property.\n\nThis statement of total ownership also settles the question of ability. Someone who doubts resurrection often doubts whether it can be done. The One who owns and sustains the heavens and the earth is not short of power to gather people again, and the surah returns to this argument repeatedly, asking who provides, who originates creation and repeats it, and who guides to the truth.\n\n\"Surely Allah's promise is ˹always˺ true\" then names what is being guaranteed. The Arabic word \"wa'd\" means a promise, and in the Quran it covers both the good news given to the believers and the warning issued to the deniers. Both are the same promise in the sense that both rest on the same divine truthfulness. A promise is only as reliable as the one who makes it: he must know what will happen, be able to bring it about, and never lie. All three are perfectly true of Allah alone, which is why the Quran elsewhere asks who is more truthful than Allah in speech.\n\nThe repetition of \"surely\" at the start of both statements is a feature of the Arabic, which uses an emphatic particle in each clause. The emphasis suits the situation, since the audience being addressed treated the promise as a fantasy.\n\n\"But most of them do not know\" identifies the real barrier once more. It is not that the promise is unclear or the evidence hidden. It is that most people never seriously examine the matter. They live inside their assumptions, which is exactly the accusation made earlier in this surah, that most follow nothing but inherited guesswork while assumption can never take the place of truth.",
  "56": "\"He ˹is the One Who˺ gives life and causes death\" answers the doubt about resurrection with something every person has already witnessed. Life appearing where there was none, and life departing from a body that remains, are events happening constantly all around us. Nobody understands how the first spark of life arrives, and nobody can hold it back when it leaves. Both are under a control that no human being shares.\n\nThe order of the two words is worth noticing. Giving life comes first, then causing death. The Quran often arranges it this way because it follows the actual sequence of a person's experience, and because it quietly makes the case for the return: the One who produced life out of nothing certainly finds it no harder to restore it. Earlier in this surah the same argument is put as a question about the associate-gods, whether any of them can originate creation and then resurrect it, with the answer that only Allah does so.\n\nDeath is also being attributed to Allah directly, which changes how a believer views it. Death is not an accident, a random failure, or the final victory of illness. It is an act of the same Lord who gave life, occurring at the appointed term mentioned a few verses earlier, which cannot be delayed or advanced. That understanding does not remove grief, but it removes the sense that the universe is out of control.\n\n\"And to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned\" completes the arc. Life is not a closed circle running from birth to burial; it is a journey with a destination. The phrase is addressed to everyone, believer and denier alike, since return is not optional and does not depend on belief in it.\n\nPlaced right after the statement that Allah's promise is always true, this verse turns a general principle into something personal. The promise is not about distant nations only. It concerns each individual listener, whose own life, death, and return are all held in the same hand.",
  "57": "\"O humanity!\" widens the address after several verses aimed at the deniers of Mecca. The Quran here is not speaking to one tribe, one city, or one generation. It calls out to every human being, and this fits the theme running through the surah, that Allah is \"Lord of all worlds\" and that every community has received a warner.\n\nFour descriptions of the revelation follow, and they form a sequence. First, \"there has come to you a warning from your Lord.\" The Arabic word \"maw'izah\" means an admonition that stirs the heart, advice given with concern, the sort of counsel a caring parent gives rather than a cold rule read out in court. It softens a person before instructing him.\n\nSecond, it is \"a cure for what is in the hearts.\" The word \"shifa'\" means healing. What is being healed is named precisely: not what is in the body, but what is in the chests, meaning doubt, envy, arrogance, hatred, hypocrisy, and attachment to falsehood. These are illnesses that no medicine reaches. The Quran describes hearts as capable of sickness, hardness, rust, and blindness, and it presents itself as the remedy for exactly those conditions. In Surah Fussilat the same word appears with a warning attached, that the Quran is \"a guide and a healing to the believers,\" while those who refuse have deafness in their ears and blindness to it. Healing depends on the patient taking the medicine.\n\nThird, it is \"a guide.\" Once a heart is cleaned, it needs a direction, and guidance shows the road to God through belief, worship, and conduct.\n\nFourth, it is \"a mercy for the believers.\" Mercy is the fruit of the whole process, appearing as peace in this life and forgiveness in the next.\n\nThe order is worth pausing over: advice, then healing, then direction, then mercy. People often want the Quran to solve outward problems before inward ones. This verse suggests the opposite order. Fix what is in the chest, and the rest of the journey becomes possible.",
  "58": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"In Allah's grace and mercy let them rejoice\"\" tells people what deserves celebration, immediately after the previous verse listed what has been given: warning, healing, guidance, and mercy. The natural response to such a gift is joy, and here joy is not merely permitted but commanded. Islam is often imagined as a religion of restraint alone, yet the Quran instructs believers to be glad, provided the gladness is attached to the right thing.\n\nEarly Muslim scholars discussed what \"grace and mercy\" refer to. Several reports from the Companions and the generation after them, including statements attributed to Ibn Abbas, explain the grace as the Quran and the mercy as Islam or faith, while others reversed the two or read them more broadly. The differences are small, and they all point in the same direction: the greatest favour a person can receive is guidance itself.\n\n\"That is far better than whatever ˹wealth˺ they amass\" draws the comparison that gives the verse its force. The Arabic word behind \"amass\" carries the sense of gathering and hoarding, piling up what one already has. Mecca's leaders measured worth by exactly that: caravans, herds, and influence. The verse does not deny that wealth is enjoyable; it says the comparison is not close. One is temporary and must be left behind, the other is permanent and travels with its owner.\n\nThere is a quiet correction here of a mistake believers can fall into. It is easy to feel small when standing beside people with far more money, and to forget what has already been received. The verse re-sets the scale. A person who has been guided has been handed something the wealthiest denier does not possess and cannot buy.\n\nThe practical effect is a change in what makes a person happy. Someone who genuinely absorbs this verse will feel real delight at learning a new part of the Quran, or at being able to pray with presence of heart, and will find the constant comparison of possessions losing its grip. Joy is not removed; it is redirected to what will last.",
  "59": "\"Ask ˹the pagans, O Prophet˺, \"Have you seen that which Allah has sent down for you as a provision, of which you have made some lawful and some unlawful?\"\" turns from the great gift of revelation to a specific abuse: people inventing religious rules and attaching them to God. The Arabs of that time had built an elaborate system of food and livestock taboos. Certain camels were dedicated to idols and could not be ridden, certain animals were forbidden to women but allowed to men, and certain crops were set aside for their gods. None of this came from any prophet. It came from custom, hardened into religion over generations.\n\nThe question begins by reminding them who actually provides. The provision was \"sent down\" by Allah, meaning it comes from Him whether directly as rain or through the chain of causes He controls. If He is the provider, only He has the right to say what may be taken and what may not. A guest who is fed in someone's house does not get to rewrite the host's rules.\n\n\"Say, \"Has Allah given you authorization, or are you fabricating lies against Allah?\"\" narrows the matter to two options, and there is no third. Either they possess a genuine permission from God, which would require revelation, or they are inventing. Since they had no scripture supporting these taboos, the answer was obvious even to them.\n\nThe principle here goes far beyond ancient camel customs. Declaring something forbidden that God has permitted, or permitted what He has forbidden, is not a small error. It is speaking on God's behalf without knowledge, which the Quran treats as one of the gravest offences. Surah an-Nahl warns against saying about what your tongues describe as falsehood, this is lawful and this is unlawful, in order to fabricate lies against Allah.\n\nThe relevance today is direct. Religious rulings require evidence, not inherited habit, personal taste, or cultural pressure. A believer who is unsure about a matter is safer saying \"I do not know\" than confidently assigning his own opinion to God.",
  "60": "\"What do those who fabricate lies against Allah expect on Judgment Day?\" is a question left deliberately hanging, and its silence is the point. The previous verse asked whether their food taboos came from divine permission or from invention. Now the follow-up asks what such inventors imagine will happen when they stand before the One they lied about. There is no answer they could give. They never thought that far, because people who make up religion are usually thinking about influence, custom, or convenience in the present, not about a reckoning.\n\nThe Arabic word \"yaftaruna,\" to fabricate, means constructing a falsehood and dressing it up as truth. Lying about a person is bad enough; lying about God, and about what He commands and forbids, misleads everyone who trusts the liar. In a well-known hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, the Prophet warned that whoever deliberately attributes a lie to him should take his seat in the Fire. If that is the case for lying about the Messenger, inventing rules and attributing them to Allah is graver still.\n\n\"Surely Allah is ever Bountiful to humanity\" changes the tone in an unexpected way. Rather than pronouncing punishment immediately, the verse points to God's generosity. The word \"fadl\" means favour and bounty, giving beyond what is required or deserved. This connects back to the discussion of provision. He gave the food they then restricted; He gave the guidance they then replaced. Even His delay in punishing them is part of that bounty, since it leaves the door of repentance open.\n\n\"But most of them are ungrateful\" identifies the response He receives. The word for ingratitude and disbelief in Arabic share the same root, \"kufr,\" whose basic sense is to cover something over. Ingratitude covers a gift and pretends it was never given; disbelief covers the Giver Himself. The two are versions of the same act.\n\nReading the verse as a whole, the arrangement is striking. Human beings misuse His gifts and speak lies in His name, and the verse responds by mentioning His generosity. That contrast is itself part of the argument.",
  "61": "\"There is no activity you may be engaged in ˹O Prophet˺ or portion of the Quran you may be reciting, nor any deed you ˹all˺ may be doing except that We are a Witness over you while doing it\" gathers everything a person can do into one statement and places it under divine observation. The address begins with the Prophet, then widens to include everyone, which shows that no one is outside the scope.\n\nThree things are mentioned, and the choice is meaningful. First, any general \"activity,\" meaning ordinary business and daily affairs. Second, \"portion of the Quran you may be reciting,\" which is worship at its most sacred. Third, \"any deed\" performed by anyone at all. Work, worship, and everyday action are all seen equally. People instinctively separate their lives into a religious part, where they behave carefully, and a normal part, where they relax their standards. The verse dissolves that division.\n\nThe phrase \"while doing it\" adds precision. The witnessing is not a report read afterwards; it is presence at the moment itself, including the intention forming quietly inside. This is close to what the Prophet described as \"ihsan\" in the well-known hadith of Gabriel recorded in Sahih Muslim, that you worship Allah as though you see Him, and though you do not see Him, He surely sees you.\n\n\"Not ˹even˺ an atom's weight is hidden from your Lord on earth or in heaven; nor anything smaller or larger than that\" then removes any idea that only big matters are recorded. The word \"dharrah\" refers to the tiniest particle imaginable, what the Arabs pictured as the smallest speck of dust visible in a shaft of sunlight. Even smaller than that is included. Surah az-Zalzalah applies the same measure to the final reckoning, where whoever does an atom's weight of good or evil will see it.\n\n\"But is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record\" completes the picture with a written account that misses nothing. For a wrongdoer this is a warning. For a believer struggling quietly, unnoticed by anyone, it is a comfort: the small kindness no one saw was not lost.",
  "62": "\"There will certainly be no fear for the close servants of Allah, nor will they grieve\" introduces one of the most quoted promises in the Quran, and it arrives right after the reminder that every deed is witnessed and recorded. That is not a coincidence. The One who misses nothing also misses none of the striving of those who love Him.\n\nThe Arabic term is \"awliya' Allah,\" here rendered as \"the close servants of Allah.\" A \"wali\" is someone near to another, an ally, a friend, one who is protected and supported. Over the centuries this word has been surrounded by misunderstanding. In popular imagination a \"wali\" became a figure with supernatural powers, whose grave is visited and whose help is sought. The Quran itself does not describe them that way, and the very next verse defines them in the simplest possible terms, as those who are faithful and mindful of Allah. No miracles are mentioned, no special lineage, no title. Closeness to God is open to any believer who takes faith and God-consciousness seriously.\n\nThe promise removes the two burdens that weigh on the human heart. Fear looks forward, to what might come. Grief looks backward, to what has been lost. Between them they consume most of a person's inner life. The promise is not that a believer never feels a moment of worry in this world, but that on the Day when it matters most there will be nothing ahead to dread and nothing behind to mourn.\n\nA famous hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, in which Allah declares war on whoever shows enmity to a \"wali\" of His, then explains how such closeness is earned: by fulfilling what has been made obligatory, and then drawing nearer through voluntary acts until Allah loves the servant. The path described there is ordinary devotion practised sincerely, not spectacular displays.\n\nThis is worth holding on to. The nearest people to God are not necessarily the most famous or the most impressive. They are often quiet, consistent believers whose main achievement is that they kept their duty to Allah when no one was watching.",
  "63": "\"˹They are˺ those who are faithful and are mindful ˹of Him˺\" answers the question the previous verse raises. Once such an enormous promise has been made to the close servants of Allah, the immediate concern of any sincere listener is how a person qualifies. The definition given is short enough to memorise and demanding enough to occupy a lifetime.\n\nThe first part is faith: believing truly in Allah, His angels, books, messengers, the Last Day, and divine decree. This is the foundation, and without it nothing else stands, since actions take their value from the belief behind them.\n\nThe second part is captured by the Arabic word \"taqwa,\" rendered here as being mindful of Him. It is often translated as fear of God, but that alone is misleading. The root sense is protection, guarding oneself, placing a shield between oneself and harm. A person with \"taqwa\" is aware that Allah sees him, and that awareness makes him careful about what he does. Umar ibn al-Khattab is reported to have described it using the image of walking along a path full of thorns, lifting your clothes and watching your steps.\n\nPutting the two together, a close servant of Allah is someone who believes correctly and lives carefully. Belief without care becomes empty talk; care without belief becomes mere ethics without a Lord. Together they produce a life quietly oriented toward God.\n\nWhat is remarkable is how accessible this definition is. It says nothing about wealth, ancestry, race, position, or spectacular experiences. A labourer, a mother at home, a student, or a shopkeeper can meet it as fully as a scholar. This matches the Quran's principle that the most honoured with Allah are the most mindful of Him.\n\nIt also removes an excuse. If closeness to God required rare gifts, most people could reasonably say the door was shut to them. The verse leaves no such exit. What it asks for is available to everyone every day: true belief, and the constant small decisions of a person who knows he is seen.",
  "64": "\"For them is good news in this worldly life and the Hereafter\" continues the description of the close servants of Allah by naming what they receive, and it deliberately splits the reward between two worlds. The good news is not postponed entirely until after death; something of it arrives now.\n\nThe Prophet was asked what this worldly good news means. In a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi and others, he explained it as the good dream that a Muslim sees or that is seen about him. Scholars have understood this as one clear example rather than the only meaning, and they have included with it other forms of encouragement a believer experiences: the peace that comes with sincere worship, the praise of righteous people, love placed in the hearts of others toward him, and above all the mercy and glad tidings given at the time of death, which the Quran describes when angels come down to those who say their Lord is Allah and remain steadfast.\n\nThe good news of the Hereafter needs less explanation, but it is worth noting that the verse gives no details here. It leaves the promise open, in keeping with the Quranic statement that no soul knows what comfort has been hidden away for it as a reward for what it used to do.\n\n\"There is no change in the promise of Allah\" then secures the whole thing. Human promises fail for three reasons: the one who promised forgets, or he becomes unable, or he changes his mind. None of these can touch Allah. The Arabic word behind \"change\" carries the sense of substitution, swapping one thing for another. Nothing about this promise will be altered, reduced, or exchanged.\n\n\"That is ˹truly˺ the ultimate triumph\" names the result with a word, \"fawz,\" that means winning, succeeding, attaining what one hoped for while escaping what one feared. The Quran uses it for the final salvation. Every other success a person achieves is partial and temporary; this is the one worth the name, and it belongs to people whose only qualifications were faith and mindfulness.",
  "65": "\"Do not let their words grieve you ˹O Prophet˺\" turns from describing the close servants of Allah to comforting the one carrying the message. The Prophet was being mocked, accused of forgery, called a poet, a soothsayer, and a madman. Insults hurt, and the Quran does not pretend otherwise. It acknowledges the pain and then gives the reasoning that dissolves it.\n\nElsewhere the same comfort is offered with striking honesty, where Allah says that He knows the Prophet's chest tightens at what they say, and in Surah al-An'am it is stated that it is not the Prophet they deny but the signs of Allah that the wrongdoers reject. Insults aimed at a messenger are really aimed at the message.\n\n\"Surely all honour and power belongs to Allah\" gives the reason. The Arabic word \"izzah\" means might, dignity, and the strength that cannot be overcome; it is the opposite of humiliation. The mockers were people of status in Mecca, and their status is precisely what made their words sting. The verse states that real honour is not distributed by public opinion. It belongs entirely to Allah, and whatever share of it anyone holds is on loan from Him. Surah Fatir puts the practical conclusion plainly: whoever desires honour, then to Allah belongs all honour.\n\nHistory settled the matter in that generation. The men who laughed in the streets of Mecca are remembered only as opponents in the story of the man they mocked, while his name is honoured aloud around the world in every call to prayer.\n\n\"He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" closes the verse with two names chosen exactly for the situation. Every insult was heard as it was spoken, including the ones muttered in private, and the intention behind each was known. Nothing needed to be reported to Him.\n\nFor any believer facing ridicule for their faith, the verse offers a way to stand. The words of critics do not define worth, the One whose judgement counts has heard everything, and honour is not in the hands of those handing out the insults.",
  "66": "\"Certainly to Allah ˹alone˺ belong all those in the heavens and all those on the earth\" follows the statement that all honour belongs to Him by explaining the scope of His ownership. The wording here refers to conscious beings, angels, humans, and jinn, not merely objects. Even the beings that people worship beside Him are His property and His servants. This alone destroys the logic of associating partners with God, since a slave cannot be a rival to his master.\n\n\"And what do those who associate others with Allah really follow?\" is a question aimed at the foundation of their religion. What exactly are they relying on? Not revelation, since none commanded idol worship. Not evidence, since no proof supports it. Not experience, since the idols never answered.\n\nThe answer arrives immediately: \"They follow nothing but assumptions and do nothing but lie.\" The word \"zann\" means guesswork, conjecture, thinking something is probably so without solid grounds. This is the same diagnosis given earlier in the surah, that most of them follow nothing but inherited assumptions, and that assumptions can in no way replace the truth. The repetition ties the two halves of the surah together and shows that the criticism is not casual; it is the central charge against them.\n\nThere is a distinction worth making here, because \"zann\" is not always condemned in Islamic teaching. In practical matters, a person often acts on strong probability, and scholars regularly weigh evidence that yields likelihood rather than certainty. What the Quran condemns is using guesswork as the basis for beliefs about God and the unseen, where nothing but revelation can give real knowledge. Deciding on the direction of prayer by careful estimate is reasonable; deciding who deserves worship by tribal custom is not.\n\nThe verse also links assumption to lying. The two are connected in a natural sequence. A person starts with a guess, repeats it, defends it, and eventually asserts it as certain fact. At that point the guess has quietly become a lie, and this is exactly how the inherited religion of the Meccans had developed over generations.",
  "67": "\"He is the One Who has made the night for you to rest in and the day bright\" answers the previous verse's exposure of guesswork by pointing to something no one can dispute. Instead of continuing to argue about idols, the Quran directs attention to a daily experience shared by every human being alive.\n\nThe night is described in terms of its purpose for people, a time to settle, to become still, to recover. The Arabic word used for this rest, from the root of \"sakan,\" conveys stillness and tranquillity, the same root behind the word for a dwelling place where a person finds peace. Darkness is not presented as something frightening but as a mercy carefully arranged. Without it, the body would have no natural signal to stop.\n\n\"And the day bright\" names the other half of the cycle, light that makes work, travel, trade, and seeing possible. Surah an-Naba describes the pair in similar terms, making the night a covering and the day a means of livelihood. The two states are opposites, yet they alternate in perfect order, each arriving without human management. No committee schedules the sunrise.\n\nModern knowledge only deepens the point. Human bodies run on an internal clock tuned to this cycle, and disturbing it disturbs sleep, mood, digestion, and health. People who work long night shifts often struggle in exactly the ways this rhythm predicts. The arrangement described in the verse is not decorative; it is woven into how we are built.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who listen\" ends by specifying who benefits. The signs are public and constant, seen by everyone, believer and denier alike. What separates people is not access to the evidence but willingness to attend to it. The word for listening here means listening in order to take something in, which is why this verse connects back to the earlier statement that the Prophet cannot make the deaf hear.\n\nFamiliarity is the real obstacle. A person who has seen thousands of sunsets stops noticing them. The Quran keeps pointing at ordinary things precisely because familiarity has quietly emptied them of meaning.",
  "68": "\"They say, \"Allah has offspring.\"\" confronts a claim that was made by several groups: Arabs who called the angels daughters of God, and communities from earlier scriptures who spoke of a divine son. The Quran deals with all of these together, because they share one mistake, imagining God as a being who reproduces.\n\n\"Glory be to Him!\" is the immediate response. The Arabic expression \"subhanahu\" declares Allah free of every fault, deficiency, and inappropriate description. It is not merely an exclamation; it is a statement that such a claim is beneath the reality of God.\n\nThen reasons follow, one after another. \"He is the Self-Sufficient.\" The word \"al-Ghani\" means the One who is completely free of need, who lacks nothing and depends on no one. Children exist to meet needs. People desire offspring for help, for continuation, for company, for someone to carry their name after they die. Every one of those needs comes from weakness and mortality. Attributing children to God quietly attributes those weaknesses to Him.\n\n\"To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" gives a second reason. Inheritance and lineage matter because ownership passes on. Everything already belongs to Him, so there is nothing to hand down and no one to hand it to. Everything in existence is His creation and His property, which makes the parent-child relationship impossible between the Creator and anything created.\n\n\"You have no proof of this!\" turns to the evidence, and finds none. Such a claim about God could only be known through revelation, and no revelation ever taught it. It rests on inherited assumption, the same problem named repeatedly in this surah.\n\n\"Do you say about Allah what you do not know?\" ends with a rebuke that is broader than the specific claim. Speaking about God without knowledge is treated in the Quran as an extremely serious matter, listed among the gravest prohibitions. The principle applies well beyond this issue: describing Allah, or declaring what He wants and forbids, requires evidence, not confident opinion.",
  "69": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Indeed, those who fabricate lies against Allah will never succeed.\"\" delivers the verdict on the claim just examined. Those who said Allah has offspring were shown to have no proof, and this verse names what such invention really is and where it leads.\n\nThe Arabic word for success here is \"yuflihun,\" from a root connected to a farmer cutting into the soil. Success in this sense is not luck or sudden gain; it is the result of working the ground, planting, and eventually harvesting. The verse says the harvest will never come for those who build their religion on lies about God, no matter how promising the early growth looks.\n\nThat last point deserves attention, because the statement seems to conflict with what people observe. Liars often prosper. The leaders of Mecca who invented their taboos and their divine daughters were wealthy and respected, while the Muslims were persecuted and poor. The Quran is not denying this. It is denying real success, which includes the end of the matter, not just the middle. A business that reports profits while quietly running up unpayable debt is not succeeding; it is only postponing the accounting.\n\nThe absolute wording, \"will never succeed,\" covers all times and all forms of fabrication. Elsewhere the Quran asks who is more unjust than the one who invents a lie about Allah, a question repeated in several places and always answered by implication: no one.\n\nIt is worth being clear about what this does and does not cover. The verse is aimed at deliberate invention, people knowingly attaching falsehood to God. It is not aimed at a sincere student who studies the evidence and reaches a conclusion that turns out to be mistaken. Islamic scholarship has always distinguished between honest error made after genuine effort and confident speech about God without knowledge.\n\nThe safeguard, then, is simple and demanding at once: when speaking about what Allah has said, commanded, or forbidden, a person should know his source, and be willing to say plainly that he does not know when he does not.",
  "70": "\"˹It is only˺ a brief enjoyment in this world\" measures the apparent prosperity of the fabricators mentioned in the previous verse. Their success was real enough to see, and that is precisely what tempted people to follow them. The verse does not deny the enjoyment; it puts a size on it. The Arabic word \"mata'\" refers to something you use for a while and then leave behind, like goods packed for a journey or a temporary provision. It is never used for anything permanent.\n\nThe Quran uses this word repeatedly to describe worldly life, and the effect is to change how a person views wealth and power without teaching him to despise them. A rented apartment is genuinely useful and can be comfortable, but nobody renovates it as though it were theirs forever. Trouble begins when the temporary is mistaken for the permanent.\n\n\"Then to Us is their return\" states the second stage. Enjoyment does not simply end; it is followed by a journey back to the One who gave it. This is the same theme that ran through the earlier verses about the appointed term and the promise that is always true. Nothing here is presented as a dramatic surprise. It is the ordinary structure of existence.\n\n\"Then We will make them taste the severe punishment for their disbelief\" names the third stage. The verb \"taste\" appeared earlier in this surah when the wrongdoers were told to taste the torment of eternity, and it carries the same force: direct experience replacing all the arguing. The cause is stated exactly, \"for their disbelief,\" which points back to what the passage has been describing, inventing lies about Allah, claiming He has offspring, forbidding what He permitted, and following guesswork instead of revelation.\n\nThe three stages together form a complete answer to the question of why wrongdoers seem to do well. There is a short period of enjoyment, a return, and then the reckoning. Anyone judging the outcome while still inside the first stage is reading a story by its opening page and assuming he knows the ending.",
  "71": "“Relate to them ˹O Prophet˺ the story of Noah when he said to his people, ‘O my People! If my presence and my reminders to you of Allah’s signs are unbearable to you, then ˹know that˺ I have put my trust in Allah. So devise a plot along with your associate-gods—and you do not have to be secretive about your plot—then carry it out against me without delay!’” opens the account of Noah in this surah, and the commentators note the tone of the address. The command “relate to them the story of Noah,” or “watlu ‘alayhim naba’a Noohin ith qala li-qawmihi,” is the summons to recite the history of the first messenger after Adam, and the verse records the moment at which Noah, after generations of patient calling, confronted his people with a declaration that revealed the depth of their hostility and the firmness of his trust. Ibn Kathir explains the background: Noah had called his people for centuries, and his presence among them had become, in their eyes, a burden, so that the mere reminder of the signs of God was “unbearable,” and the verse records the people’s condition through Noah’s own words, the weariness of a people who could no longer endure the message.\n\nThe declaration that Noah made is the verse’s heart: “I have put my trust in Allah,” or “fa-‘ala Allahi tawakkaltu,” the “tawakkul” that Noah announced as his only defense, and the challenge that followed: “So devise a plot along with your associate-gods… then carry it out against me without delay,” or “fa-ajmi‘oo amrakum wa shuraka’akum… thumma iqdoo ilayya wa la tunziroon.” Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari note the audacity of the challenge: the prophet who stood alone against his people invited them to gather their plots and their gods and to do their worst without delay, and the invitation was not the bravado of the reckless but the courage of the one who had entrusted his affair to the Lord, for the one who trusts in God fears no plot of the creatures. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the complete meaning of the trust: the “tawakkul” is not the abandonment of the effort but the surrender of the outcome, and the one who has surrendered the outcome can invite the whole world to plot against him, for the plots of the world cannot touch the one whom the Lord protects. The verse is the portrait of the trust of Noah, and the summons to the same trust for every believer who faces the hostility of the heedless.",
  "72": "“And if you turn away, ˹remember˺ I have never demanded a reward from you ˹for delivering the message˺. My reward is only from Allah. And I have been commanded to be one of those who submit ˹to Allah˺.”” continues Noah’s address, and the commentators note the two declarations that the verse joins. The first is the disavowal of the reward: “I have never demanded a reward from you for delivering the message,” or “in sa’altukum ajran fa-ajri illa ‘ala Allahi,” and the commentators explain the significance of this declaration, for it is the refrain of every prophet in the Quran, the assurance that the message is not a trade and the messenger is not a merchant, and the one who delivers the word of God does not seek the payment of the people, for his reward is with the One who sent him. Ibn Kathir notes the repetition of this declaration across the prophetic stories: Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu‘ayb all declared that they sought no reward, and the repetition is the proof that the accusation of self-interest, which the people of every age hurl at the messengers, is the oldest and falsest of the charges. The second declaration is the submission: “And I have been commanded to be one of those who submit,” or “wa umirtu an akoona mina al-muslimeen,” the statement of the prophet’s own surrender, the “islam” that the verse names, and the commentators observe the beauty of the declaration, for the messenger who calls others to the submission is himself the first of the submitters, and the one who commands the worship of God is the one who has already surrendered his own will to the One he worships.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the freedom of the messenger, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The one who delivers the truth and seeks no reward from the people is the one who is free, for the messenger who takes no payment owes no obligation, and the one who seeks the reward only from God is the one whose tongue cannot be bought and whose message cannot be silenced by the withholding of the world’s goods. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the dignity of the caller: the reward that is sought from God is the reward that no creature can withhold, and the messenger who has placed his reward with his Lord is the messenger whom no bribe can corrupt and no threat can bend. The verse is the declaration of the prophet’s independence, and the model of the caller who serves the Lord alone.",
  "73": "“But they still rejected him, so We saved him and those with him in the Ark and made them successors, and drowned those who rejected Our signs. See then what was the end of those who had been warned!” closes the account of Noah with the verdict of the flood, and the commentators note the sequence of the three outcomes. The verse states the rejection first: “But they still rejected him,” or “fa-kaththaboohu,” the persistence of the denial despite the centuries of the call, and then the three outcomes that followed: the salvation, “so We saved him and those with him in the Ark,” or “fa-najjaynahu wa man ma‘ahu fi al-fulk,” the rescue of Noah and the believers aboard the ship; the succession, “and made them successors,” or “wa ja‘alnahum khala’if,” the establishment of the saved as the heirs of the earth; and the drowning, “and drowned those who rejected Our signs,” or “wa aghraqna allatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina,” the destruction of the deniers in the flood. Ibn Kathir explains the fuller account: Noah called his people for nine hundred and fifty years, and only a small band believed, and when the decree came, the fountains of the earth burst and the sky poured, and the Ark carried the believers while the flood swept the deniers away, and the succession of the saved was the beginning of the new humanity that the earth would bear.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that turns the account into a lesson: “See then what was the end of those who had been warned!” or “fanzur kayfa kana ‘aqibatu al-munthareen,” the summons to the Prophet and to every listener to reflect upon the end of the warned who rejected the warning, and the commentators note the force of the question, for the end of the warned is the warning itself, and the one who sees the end of the deniers of Noah is the one who is warned against the same denial. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the complete instance of the pattern of the surah: the messenger called, the people rejected, the few were saved, and the many were drowned, and the question “see then what was the end” is the Quran’s method of turning the history into the present, the summons to the listener to read his own condition in the fate of the warned. The verse is the verdict of the flood, and the teaching that the end of the warned is the measure of the warning.",
  "74": "\"Then after him We sent ˹other˺ messengers to their ˹own˺ people\" moves from Noah forward through the long stretch of history that followed. Rather than telling each story in detail here, the Quran summarises an entire era. Prophets were sent to Ad, to Thamud, to Madyan, and to communities whose names are not recorded, each one raised from among the people he addressed so that no one could claim the messenger was a stranger who did not understand them.\n\n\"And they came to them with clear proofs\" describes what those messengers brought. The Arabic word \"bayyinat\" means clear evidences, things that make the truth obvious and remove excuses. These included miracles, but also the clarity of the message itself and the character of the men delivering it. Nobody was asked to believe on a hunch.\n\n\"But they would not believe in what they had rejected before\" is the striking part of the verse. It says the refusal was already settled before the evidence arrived. Their minds had been made up by their fathers' religion, by their interests, by their pride, and the proofs then had to fight against a decision already taken. Anyone who has argued with a person whose position was fixed in advance recognises this. Facts bounce off.\n\n\"This is how We seal the hearts of the transgressors\" explains what happens next, and this phrase needs care, because it is often misread. It is not saying that God closes hearts arbitrarily and then blames people for being closed. The order in the verse is the opposite: they rejected first, repeatedly and knowingly, and the sealing came after as the settled consequence. It works like a muscle that stiffens from disuse, or a door hinge that rusts shut because it was never opened.\n\nThe word \"mu'tadin,\" rendered here as transgressors, means those who go past the proper limits. Sealing is described as the outcome for people who habitually cross the line, not for those who stumble.\n\nThe warning in this for anyone is practical. Refusing what you know to be right is never a neutral act; each refusal makes the next acceptance a little harder.",
  "75": "“Then after these ˹messengers˺ We sent Moses and Aaron to Pharaoh and his chiefs with Our signs. But they behaved arrogantly and were a wicked people.” opens the account of Moses in this surah, and the commentators note its place in the sequence. The verse says “after these messengers,” or “thumma ba‘athna min ba‘dihim,” placing the mission of Moses after the line of the earlier prophets, and it names the two brothers: “We sent Moses and Aaron,” or “Moosa wa Haroon,” the two who were sent together, and the recipients: “to Pharaoh and his chiefs,” or “ila Fir‘awna wa mala’ihi,” the tyrant and his ruling class, and the credentials: “with Our signs,” or “bi-ayatina,” the miracles and the proofs that God had given them. Ibn Kathir explains the significance of the pairing: Moses was the messenger, and Aaron was his supporter and his minister, and the two were sent to the greatest tyrant of the age with the signs that the following verses describe, the staff and the hand and the plagues, and the mission of the two brothers was the confrontation of the truth with the might of Egypt. The verse then states the response: “But they behaved arrogantly and were a wicked people,” or “fastakbaroo wa kanoo qawman mujrimeen,” the arrogance of Pharaoh and his chiefs and the wickedness of their state, and the commentators note that the two words — the “istikbar,” the arrogance, and the “ijram,” the wickedness — are the two roots of the rejection, the pride that refused the truth and the crime that the pride produced.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the recurring pattern, and the commentators connect it to the whole surah. The mission of Moses is the culmination of the sequence of the messengers, and the response of Pharaoh is the culmination of the sequence of the rejections, and the verse teaches that the confrontation between the truth and the tyranny is the permanent drama of the history, the messenger sent and the tyrant refusing, and the outcome of the drama, which the following verses record, is the outcome that the believer trusts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse sets the stage for the full account of Moses that follows, and that the believer reads in it the principle that governs every confrontation: the arrogance of the powerful is the prelude of their fall, and the signs of God, however resisted, are the instruments of the victory that the arrogance cannot prevent. The verse is the opening of the account, and the statement of the two forces that the account will pit against each other.",
  "76": "“When the truth came to them from Us, they said, “This is certainly pure magic!”” records the first response of Pharaoh and his chiefs to the signs, and the commentators note the evasion that the accusation reveals. The verse states the arrival of the truth: “When the truth came to them from Us,” or “fa-lamma ja’ahumu al-haqqu min ‘indina,” and the commentators explain the phrase, for the signs that Moses brought were not the arguments of a man but the truth of God, the “haqq” that had come “from Us,” and the arrival of the truth is the point of the verse, for the people were not faced with a claim but with a reality. Then the response: “they said, ‘This is certainly pure magic!’” or “qaloo inna hatha la-sihrun mubeen,” the accusation of the sorcery, the “sihr mubeen,” the clear magic, and the commentators note the evasion: the chiefs could not deny the signs, for the staff and the hand had been witnessed, and so they classified them, calling the truth magic in order to avoid calling it truth, and the classification was the refuge of the one who had no answer. Ibn Kathir explains the psychology: the one who cannot refute the truth renames it, and the renaming of the truth as magic is the oldest of the rejecters’ strategies, the attempt to escape the evidence by the label, and the label changes nothing, for the staff that became a serpent remained the truth that the accusation could not undo.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the nature of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own experience. The truth, when it comes, is always met first by the accusation of the false, and the believer who carries the truth must expect the label of the magician, the charge that the light is the deception, and the expectation is the preparation, for the accusation of the truth as magic is the tribute that the falsehood pays to the power that it cannot match. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the enemy’s weapon: the truth is called magic in every age, and the calling is the confession of the caller, for the one who must call the light a deception has already admitted that the light is beyond his power to extinguish. The verse is the record of the evasion, and the warning against the label that the falsehood affixes to the truth.",
  "77": "“Moses responded, “Is this what you say about the truth when it has come to you? Is this magic? Magicians will never succeed.”” records Moses’ answer to the accusation, and the commentators note the three points of the reply. The first is the question: “Is this what you say about the truth when it has come to you?” or “a-taqooloona lil-haqqi lamma ja’akum a-sihrun hatha,” the exposure of the absurdity of the accusation, for the truth that had come from God was being called magic, and the question placed the absurdity before the accusers, asking them whether the reality that they had witnessed could be dismissed by the word they had chosen. Ibn Kathir explains the force of the question: Moses did not argue the point but asked the question that the accusers themselves could not answer honestly, for the truth that had come to them was not the illusion that the word “magic” implied, and the question was the first blow against the label. The second is the further question: “Is this magic?” or “a-sihrun hatha,” the repetition that underscored the absurdity, and the third is the verdict: “Magicians will never succeed,” or “wa la yuflihu al-sahiroon,” the declaration that the magicians, the practitioners of the illusion, never prosper, and the commentators note the meaning, for the “falah,” the success, is the reward of the truthful, and the magician, whose art is the deception, is excluded from it, and the declaration is both the description of the magicians and the answer to the accusation, for the one who calls the truth magic has placed himself among the magicians whose failure the verse declares.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the calm of the messenger, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own response to the accusation. Moses did not rage against the accusers but answered the accusation with the question and the verdict, and the calm of the answer is the calm of the one who knows the outcome, for the truth does not need the defense of the anger, and the magician, whatever his art, is the one whose end is the failure that the verse declares. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the reply to the false charge: the truth is defended by the question that exposes the absurdity and the verdict that states the outcome, and the one who answers the accusation with the anger has forgotten that the outcome is already decided. The verse is the model of the calm reply, and the declaration of the failure of the falsehood.",
  "78": "“They argued, “Have you come to turn us away from the faith of our forefathers so that the two of you may become supreme in the land? We will never believe in you!”” records the deeper objection of the chiefs, and the commentators note the two accusations that the verse joins. The first is the accusation of the innovation: “Have you come to turn us away from the faith of our forefathers,” or “a-ji’tana li-talfitana ‘amma wajadna ‘alayhi aba’ana,” the charge that the message of Moses was an assault upon the religion of the ancestors, and the commentators explain the significance, for the appeal to the forefathers is the oldest of the rejecters’ arguments, the claim that the inherited is the true and the new is the false, and the verse exposes the argument, for the faith of the forefathers was the idolatry that the message had come to correct, and the correction of the ancestors is not the betrayal of them but the mercy upon them. The second accusation is the political: “so that the two of you may become supreme in the land,” or “wa takoona lakuma al-kibriya’u fi al-ardi,” the charge that Moses and Aaron sought the dominion, the “kibriya’,” the greatness, the supremacy, and the commentators note the irony, for the men who accused the two brothers of seeking the dominion were the men who held it, and the accusation was the projection of their own ambition upon the messengers. Then the conclusion: “We will never believe in you!” or “wa ma nahnu lakuma bi-mu’mineen,” the absolute refusal, and Ibn Kathir notes the finality of the declaration, for the chiefs had closed the door of the belief before the signs could be examined.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two accusations, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own trials. The message of the truth is always accused of the two things — the attack upon the inherited and the ambition for the power — and the two accusations are the two faces of the single fear, the fear of the one whose position the truth threatens, and the believer who carries the truth must expect both, the charge of the innovation and the charge of the ambition, and must answer both with the purity of the intention that the message itself declares. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the opposition: the truth is opposed not because it is false but because it is costly, and the chiefs who accused Moses of the ambition were the chiefs whose own ambition the truth had exposed. The verse is the record of the objection, and the warning against the two accusations that the truth always faces.",
  "79": "“Pharaoh demanded, “Bring me every skilled magician.”” shows the ruler’s next move once argument failed, and the commentators note the significance of the demand. Pharaoh, having heard the accusation of the magic from his chiefs and the reply of Moses, now acted, and his action was the summoning of the magicians: “Bring me every skilled magician,” or “i’toonee bi-kulli sahirin ‘aleem,” and the commentators explain the demand, for the tyrant, unable to answer the signs of Moses with the argument, resolved to answer them with the craft, and the gathering of the magicians was the gathering of the falsehood’s champions against the truth, the assembly of the “sahir ‘aleem,” the magician of the skill, the master of the illusion, from all the cities of Egypt. Ibn Kathir describes the scene: Egypt was the centre of the magical arts, and its sorcerers were the pride of the land, and Pharaoh summoned the best of them to confront Moses, confident that the craft that had dazzled the people would undo the signs that had come with the two brothers, and the summoning was the preparation of the great contest that the following verses describe.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the tyrant’s method, and the commentators connect it to the falsehood’s strategy in every age. The truth is answered first with the accusation, then with the assembly of the experts, and the tyrant who cannot refute the message gathers the professionals of the age to overwhelm it, and the gathering is the admission that the argument has failed, for the one who must summon the champions has already confessed that he has no answer of his own. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nature of the falsehood’s power: it is the power of the assembly, the weight of the numbers and the skill, and the truth is the power of the one, the sign that no assembly can match, and the contest of the magicians and Moses is the contest of the two powers, the assembly of the falsehood against the sign of the truth. The verse is the record of the summoning, and the preparation of the confrontation that the truth would win.",
  "80": "“When the magicians came, Moses said to them, “Cast whatever you wish to cast!”” captures the composure of the messenger before the assembled craft, and the commentators note the confidence of the invitation. The verse describes the arrival of the magicians and the challenge of Moses: “When the magicians came,” or “fa-lamma ja’a as-saharatu,” the gathering of the sorcerers before Pharaoh and the people, and then the invitation: “Moses said to them, ‘Cast whatever you wish to cast!’” or “qala lahum Moosa alqoo ma antum mulqoon,” and the commentators note the audacity of the invitation, for Moses did not hesitate before the assembled champions but invited them to display their full art, and the invitation was the confidence of the one who knows that the truth needs no advantage, and that the falsehood, when it has done its worst, has only prepared the stage for the sign that will undo it. Ibn Kathir explains the significance: the invitation to cast first was the surrender of the first move to the enemy, and the surrender was the strategy of the truth, for the display of the magic in its fullness would make the swallowing of it the more decisive, and the people who saw the full power of the falsehood would see the fuller power of the truth.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the composure, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer who stands for the truth is the believer who can invite the falsehood to do its worst, for the truth does not fear the display of the illusion, and the one who trusts the Lord of the truth can let the enemy cast first, knowing that the last word belongs to the truth that the enemy cannot match. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the calm of the certain: Moses stood before the magicians with the calm of the one who had seen the staff and the hand, and his invitation to cast was the invitation of the one whose outcome was already written, and the believer who has the same certainty can face the assembled falsehood of his age with the same composure. The verse is the portrait of the confident messenger, and the summons to the same confidence for every believer.",
  "81": "“When they did, Moses said, “What you have produced is mere magic, Allah will surely make it useless, for Allah certainly does not set right the work of the corruptors.”” records Moses’ verdict upon the display, and the commentators note the two parts of the sentence. The verse describes the casting: “When they did,” or “fa-lamma alqaw,” the throwing of the ropes and the staffs, the display of the magic in its fullness, and then the verdict of Moses: “What you have produced is mere magic, Allah will surely make it useless,” or “qala Moosa ma ji’tum bihi as-sihru inna Allaha sa-yubtiluhu,” and the commentators explain the verdict, for Moses declared that what the magicians had produced was the “sihr,” the magic, the illusion, and that God would make it “batil,” useless, null, of no effect, and the declaration was the prophecy of the outcome that the next verse records, the swallowing of the magic by the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the precision of the words: Moses did not call the display by any other name but “sihr,” the illusion, and he declared its nullity in advance, and the declaration was the certainty of the one who knew that the truth was about to swallow the falsehood. The second part is the principle: “for Allah certainly does not set right the work of the corruptors,” or “inna Allaha la yuslihu ‘amala al-mufsideen,” the declaration that the work of the “mufsideen,” the corruptors, is never set right, never made to prosper, and the commentators note the application, for the magicians were the corruptors, and their work, however dazzling, was the work that God does not set right, and the failure of the work is the law that the verse declares.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the verdict, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s judgment of the falsehood. The believer is taught to name the falsehood by its name, to call the illusion the illusion, and to declare its nullity with the certainty of the outcome, and the one who declares the work of the corruptors useless is the one who has aligned his tongue with the decree of God, for the work of the corruptors is always the work that God makes useless, however long it appears to prosper. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the verdict: the falsehood is named, its end is declared, and the declaration is the faith that the believer speaks before the outcome is seen, and the outcome that follows is the proof that the declaration was true. The verse is the record of the verdict, and the declaration of the law that the falsehood cannot escape.",
  "82": "“And Allah establishes the truth by His Words—even to the dismay of the wicked.” states the positive side of what the previous verse had declared, and the commentators note the two clauses and their meaning. The first clause is the establishment: “And Allah establishes the truth by His Words,” or “wa yuhiqqqu Allahu al-haqqa bi-kalimatihi,” and the commentators explain the phrase, for the truth is established by the “kalimat,” the words of God, the decrees and the revelations and the commands, and the establishment of the truth is the divine act by which the falsehood is made null, and the words of God are the instruments of the establishment, the decrees that the truth speaks and the falsehood cannot answer. Ibn Kathir explains the application: the truth of Moses was established at the contest by the word of God, the swallowing of the magic by the staff, and the establishment was the proof that the truth does not need the numbers or the craft, for the word of God alone is sufficient to establish it and to nullify the falsehood. The second clause is the qualification: “even to the dismay of the wicked,” or “wa law kariha al-mujrimoon,” the establishment of the truth regardless of the dislike of the “mujrimoon,” the criminals, the wicked, and the commentators note the force of the qualification, for the truth is established whether the wicked like it or not, and the dismay of the wicked is the measure of the triumph of the truth, the tribute that the falsehood pays to the truth it cannot stop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two words, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s confidence. The truth and the falsehood are both spoken, and the word of the truth is the word that God establishes, while the word of the falsehood is the word that God nullifies, and the believer who speaks the word of the truth is the believer whose word is backed by the establishment of God, and the wicked who hate the establishment are the ones whose dismay is the proof of the triumph. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the assurance of the believer against the hatred of the falsehood: the truth is established by the words of God, and the dismay of the wicked is the sign that the establishment has come, and the one who stands with the word of the truth is the one who stands with what God establishes. The verse is the statement of the establishment, and the assurance that the truth prevails despite the wicked.",
  "83": "“But no one believed in Moses except a few youths of his people, while fearing that Pharaoh and their own chiefs might persecute them. And certainly Pharaoh was a tyrant in the land, and he was truly a transgressor.” reports the response to everything Moses had shown, and the commentators note the two facts that the verse records. The first is the scarcity of the believers: “no one believed in Moses except a few youths of his people,” or “fa-ma amana li-Moosa illa thurriyyatun min qawmihi,” and the commentators explain the word “thurriyyah,” the offspring, the young, and the meaning, for the believers were a small band of the young, the few who had the courage that the elders had lost, and the scarcity of the believers is the measure of the terror that the truth faced, for the message of Moses, after all the signs, had won only the few. Ibn Kathir explains the detail: the believers were a small group of the youths of the Children of Israel, and their belief was secret, for they feared the persecution that the verse names. The second fact is the fear: “while fearing that Pharaoh and their own chiefs might persecute them,” or “‘ala khawfin min Fir‘awna wa mala’ihim an yaftinahum,” the fear of the “fitnah,” the persecution, the trial, and the commentators note the reality of the fear, for Pharaoh was the tyrant that the verse describes: “And certainly Pharaoh was a tyrant in the land, and he was truly a transgressor,” or “wa inna Fir‘awna la-‘alin fi al-ardi wa innahu la-mina al-musrifin,” the “‘alin,” the high-handed, the dominant, and the “musrif,” the transgressor, the one who exceeded every bound.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the courage of the few, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own minority. The believers of Moses were the few and the young, and their fear was real, and yet their belief was the seed of the deliverance that the following verses record, and the believer is taught that the scarcity of the believers is not the failure of the truth, for the truth is the truth whether one believes or a thousand, and the few who believe in the face of the tyrant are the ones whom the Lord delivers and honours. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the struggle: the truth is often carried by the few, and the few are often the young and the weak, and their fear is the companion of their faith, and the deliverance that follows is the reward of the courage that believed despite the fear. The verse is the record of the few, and the honour of the minority that the truth could not fail.",
  "84": "“Moses said, “O my people! If you do believe in Allah and submit ˹to His Will˺, then put your trust in Him.”” records the counsel of Moses to the believing few, and the commentators note the condition and the command. Moses addressed the believers with the word of kinship: “O my people!” or “ya qawmi,” and then the condition: “If you do believe in Allah and submit to His Will,” or “in kuntum amantum billahi fa-‘alayhi tawakkalu in kuntum muslimeen,” and the command that follows: “then put your trust in Him,” or “fa-‘alayhi tawakkalu,” and the commentators explain the logic of the sentence, for the trust in God is the consequence of the belief and the submission, and the one who truly believes and submits is the one whose heart rests in the trust, and the counsel of Moses was the preparation of the believers for the trial, the instruction to place their trust in the Lord in the face of the tyranny of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir explains the significance: the believers were few and afraid, and Moses called them to the trust that their fear had weakened, and the trust was the remedy of the fear, for the one who trusts the Lord does not fear the tyrant, and the command of the trust was the command of the courage.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the order of the faith and the trust, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The trust is the fruit of the belief, and the belief that does not produce the trust is the belief that has not taken root, and the believer is taught that the fear of the creature is the sign of the weak trust, while the trust in the Creator is the sign of the firm faith, and the one who believes and submits is the one whose fear is directed to the Lord alone. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the courage: the trust is not an addition to the faith but its completion, and the believer who has trusted the Lord has entrusted the outcome to the One who alone determines it, and the tyrant who threatens the body has no power over the soul that the trust has surrendered to its Lord. The verse is the counsel of the trust, and the summons of the believer to the reliance that the fear dissolves.",
  "85": "“They replied, “In Allah we trust. Our Lord! Do not subject us to the persecution of the oppressive people,”” records the answer of the believers, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the declaration: “In Allah we trust,” or “‘ala Allahi tawakkalna,” and the commentators note the immediacy of the answer, for the believers responded to the counsel of Moses without hesitation, and the response was the echo of the command, the declaration that the trust was the state of their hearts, and the word “tawakkalna” is the same word that Moses had commanded, so that the reply was the obedience of the counsel and the confession of the trust. Ibn Kathir explains the significance: the believers, few and afraid, declared their trust in the Lord, and the declaration was the turning of the hearts from the fear of the tyrant to the reliance upon the Creator, and the trust that they declared was the shield that the trial would test. The second part is the prayer: “Our Lord! Do not subject us to the persecution of the oppressive people,” or “rabbana la taj‘alna fitnatan lil-qawmi az-zalimeen,” the plea to the Lord not to make them the “fitnah,” the trial, the object of the persecution of the wrongdoers, and the commentators explain the meaning, for the believers asked that they be spared the affliction that the oppressors would inflict, and the plea was the expression of the fear that their trust had not extinguished, for the believer trusts and prays, and the two are the companions of the one who faces the trial.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration and the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own response to the danger. The believer is taught to answer the counsel of the trust with the declaration of the trust, and to follow the declaration with the prayer that seeks the protection of the Lord, and the two together are the complete posture of the believer in the trial, the heart that trusts and the tongue that prays, and the one who combines the two is the one whose fear is the fear of the Lord alone. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the language of the reliance: “in Allah we trust” is the confession of the heart, and “do not subject us to the persecution” is the prayer of the need, and the believer who speaks both has spoken the whole of the faith in the hour of the trial. The verse is the record of the answer, and the model of the believer’s reply to the counsel of the trust.",
  "86": "“and deliver us by Your mercy from the disbelieving people.”” completes the supplication begun in the previous verse, and the commentators note the two requests and their object. The verse is the second half of the prayer of the believers: “and deliver us by Your mercy from the disbelieving people,” or “wa najjina bi-rahmatika mina al-qawmi al-kafireen,” and the commentators explain the request, for the believers asked the Lord to deliver them, the “najjina,” the rescue, by His mercy, the “bi-rahmatika,” and the object of the deliverance was the disbelieving people, the “qawm al-kafireen,” the people of Pharaoh who had rejected the truth, and the prayer was the plea of the few for the rescue from the many, the cry of the believers for the salvation that only the mercy of the Lord could grant. Ibn Kathir notes the joining of the two requests: the first was the plea not to be made the persecution of the oppressors, and the second was the plea for the deliverance from the disbelievers, and the two together are the complete prayer of the believer in the trial, the asking of the protection and the asking of the rescue, and the two askings are the two hands of the supplication raised to the Lord.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy as the means, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The deliverance is asked by the mercy, and the mercy is the attribute that the believer invokes in the hour of the danger, and the verse teaches that the rescue of the believer is not the reward of the strength but the gift of the mercy, and the one who is delivered is the one who asked the mercy and received it, and the one who is lost is the one who forgot the mercy that could have saved him. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse completes the supplication of the believers, and that the believer reads in it the truth of the deliverance: the rescue from the disbelievers is the work of the mercy, and the mercy is the refuge of the believer in every age, and the prayer “deliver us by Your mercy” is the prayer that the believer never ceases to need. The verse is the completion of the prayer, and the teaching that the mercy of the Lord is the means of the deliverance.",
  "87": "“We revealed to Moses and his brother, “Appoint houses for your people in Egypt. Turn these houses into places of worship, establish prayer, and give good news to the believers!”” answers the prayer of the believers with the command of the preparation, and the commentators note the four commands and their wisdom. The verse records the revelation: “We revealed to Moses and his brother,” or “wa awhayna ila Moosa wa akheehi,” the inspiration to the two brothers, and the first command: “Appoint houses for your people in Egypt,” or “an tabawwa’a li-qawmikuma bi-Misra buyootan,” the taking of houses, and the commentators explain the command, for the believers were to establish their homes and their places of gathering, and the second command: “Turn these houses into places of worship,” or “waj‘aloo buyootakum qiblatan,” the making of the houses into “qiblah,” the places of the prayer, and Ibn Kathir explains the meaning, for the believers, who could not worship openly in the temples of Egypt, were commanded to make their homes the mosques, the private sanctuaries of the prayer. The third command: “establish prayer,” or “wa aqeemoo as-salah,” the establishment of the prayer, and the fourth: “and give good news to the believers!” or “wa bashshiri al-mu’mineen,” the glad tidings, and the commentators note the sequence, for the houses were the shelter, the prayer was the strength, and the good news was the hope, and the three together were the complete preparation of the believers for the deliverance that was coming.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the home as the sanctuary, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the home can be the mosque, and the private space can be the sanctuary, and the one who cannot worship openly in the land of the tyranny is the one who turns his house into the place of the prayer, and the prayer that is established in the home is the prayer that the Lord accepts and strengthens, and the good news of the deliverance is the reward of the steadfastness that the prayer produces. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the response to the persecution: the houses are appointed, the prayer is established, and the glad tidings are given, and the believer who follows the command is the believer who is prepared for the deliverance that the following verses record. The verse is the command of the preparation, and the teaching of the home as the sanctuary of the faith.",
  "88": "“Moses prayed, “Our Lord! You have granted Pharaoh and his chiefs luxuries and riches in this worldly life, ˹which they abused˺ to lead people astray from Your Way! Our Lord, destroy their riches and harden their hearts so that they will not believe until they see the painful punishment.”” records the prayer of Moses against the tyrant, and the commentators note the two parts of the supplication. The first is the description of the abuse: “You have granted Pharaoh and his chiefs luxuries and riches in this worldly life, which they abused to lead people astray from Your Way,” or “rabbana innaka atayta Fir‘awna wa mala’ahu zeenatan wa amwalan fi al-hayati ad-dunya… li-yudilloo ‘an sabeelika,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Moses acknowledged that the wealth and the splendour of the tyrant were the gifts of God, and that the tyrant had abused the gifts, using the wealth not for the gratitude but for the leading of the people astray, and the acknowledgment is the teaching that the worldly gifts are the test, and the abuse of the gift is the sin that the prayer condemns. The second part is the prayer: “Our Lord, destroy their riches and harden their hearts so that they will not believe until they see the painful punishment,” or “rabbana itmis ‘ala amwalihim washdud ‘ala quloobihim fa-la yu’minoo hatta yaraw al-‘athaba al-aleem,” and Ibn Kathir explains the prayer, for Moses asked that the wealth of the tyrant be blotted out and his heart sealed, so that the rejection would be complete until the punishment, and the commentators note that the prayer was the prayer of the prophet in the extremity of the confrontation, the plea for the judgment upon the one whose rejection had exhausted every mercy.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer against the oppressor, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own supplication. The believer is taught that the prayer against the oppressor is the prayer of the oppressed, and that the prayer for the judgment is the resort of the one who has exhausted the argument and the warning, and the verse records that the prayer of Moses was answered, as the next verse declares, so that the believer learns that the plea of the oppressed is not lost, and the judgment upon the tyrant is the answer that the prayer invites. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the limit of the patience: the prophet who had called and warned and shown the signs now prayed for the judgment, and the prayer was the turning of the matter to the One who alone judges, and the believer who has done his duty may pray the same prayer, for the Lord who hears the oppressed is the Lord who answers the plea. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the supplication of the oppressed.",
  "89": "“Allah responded ˹to Moses and Aaron˺, “Your prayer is answered! So be steadfast and do not follow the way of those who do not know.”” confirms the acceptance of the prayer, and the commentators note the two commands that the answer joins. The first is the confirmation: “Your prayer is answered!” or “qad ujeebat da‘watukuma,” and the commentators note the word, for the prayer of the two brothers was accepted, and the acceptance is the assurance that the plea of the oppressed is heard, and the next verses record the fulfillment of the acceptance in the drowning of Pharaoh and the deliverance of the believers. Ibn Kathir explains the significance: God answered the prayer of Moses and Aaron, and the answer was the declaration that the judgment upon the tyrant was decreed, and the believers were to wait for the fulfillment with the steadfastness that the answer commanded. The second part is the two commands: “So be steadfast and do not follow the way of those who do not know,” or “fastaqeema wa la tattabi‘anni sabeela allatheena la ya‘lamoon,” the command of the “istiqamah,” the steadfastness, the firm holding of the way, and the prohibition of the way of the ignorant, and the commentators explain the meaning, for the answer to the prayer was not the immediate fulfillment but the command to remain steadfast until the fulfillment came, and the steadfastness was the condition of the deliverance, and the way of the ignorant was the impatience and the despair that the believers were warned against.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the waiting, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the answered prayer is often followed by the waiting, and the waiting is the test of the steadfastness, and the one who is told that his prayer is answered is the one who is commanded to stand firm until the answer arrives, and the steadfastness in the waiting is the proof of the faith that prayed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the answered prayer: the acceptance is certain, and the steadfastness is the command, and the one who hears the answer and remains firm is the one whom the fulfillment finds ready, while the one who follows the way of the ignorant is the one who loses the answer in the impatience. The verse is the record of the acceptance, and the command of the steadfastness that the fulfillment requires.",
  "90": "“We brought the Children of Israel across the sea. Then Pharaoh and his soldiers pursued them unjustly and oppressively. But as Pharaoh was drowning, he cried out, “I believe that there is no god except that in whom the Children of Israel believe, and I am ˹now˺ one of those who submit.”” records the crossing and the drowning, and the commentators describe the scene and the confession. The verse states the crossing first: “We brought the Children of Israel across the sea,” or “wa jawazna bi-Banee Isra’eela al-bahr,” and the commentators note the brevity of the statement, for the fuller account of the parting of the sea is given elsewhere, and the crossing here is the deliverance that the prayer of Moses had sought. Then the pursuit: “Then Pharaoh and his soldiers pursued them unjustly and oppressively,” or “fa-atba‘ahum Fir‘awnu wa junooduhu baghyan wa ‘adwan,” the pursuit of the tyrant and his host, the “baghyan wa ‘adwan,” the injustice and the aggression, and the commentators note the two words, for the pursuit was not the defense of a right but the aggression of the wrongdoer, and the two words are the verdict upon the chase. Then the drowning and the confession: “But as Pharaoh was drowning, he cried out, ‘I believe that there is no god except that in whom the Children of Israel believe, and I am now one of those who submit,’” or “hatta itha adrakahu al-gharaqu qala amantu annahu la ilaha illa allathee amanat bihi Banu Isra’eela wa ana mina al-muslimeen,” and Ibn Kathir relates the scene: the sea closed over Pharaoh, and in the moment of the drowning he confessed the faith of the Children of Israel, the “amantu” that came too late, and the commentators note the confession and its rejection, for the faith of the drowning is not the faith of the saved, and the next verse records the answer that the confession received.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the late confession, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own urgency. The believer is taught that the faith is accepted in the life, not in the drowning, and the one who delays the belief until the moment of the death has delayed it beyond the door, and the confession of Pharaoh is the warning against the postponement of the return, for the “amantu” of the drowning is the confession that the repentance of the living should have made. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the present hour: the faith that is delayed to the last moment is the faith that the last moment rejects, and the one who would die as a submitter must live as one, and the confession of Pharaoh is the measure of the loss that the delay brings. The verse is the record of the crossing and the drowning, and the warning of the confession that came too late.",
  "91": "“˹He was told,˺ “Now ˹you believe˺? But you always disobeyed and were one of the corruptors.”” is the reply to Pharaoh’s declaration, and the commentators note the two clauses and their force. The first is the rebuke: “Now you believe?” or “al-ana,” and the commentators explain the word, for the “al-ana” is the rebuke of the one whose belief has come at the moment of the despair, and the question is the rejection of the late confession, the declaration that the faith of the drowning is not the faith that the Lord accepts. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: Pharaoh had been shown the signs and had rejected them, and his confession in the moment of the drowning was not the belief of the heart but the terror of the death, and the question “now you believe?” is the answer that the late belief receives, the rejection of the confession that the danger had wrung from the one who had refused the warning. The second clause is the indictment: “But you always disobeyed and were one of the corruptors,” or “wa qad ‘asayta qablu wa kunta mina al-mufsideen,” and the commentators note the two charges, the “isyan,” the disobedience, and the “ifsad,” the corruption, and the two together are the record of the life that the late confession could not erase, for the man who had disobeyed and corrupted through the years of the signs was the man whose deathbed confession was the fruit of the fear, not the repentance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own account. The believer is taught that the life is the record, and the confession at the end cannot erase the disobedience of the beginning, and the one who would be counted among the believers at the death must be counted among them in the life, and the record of Pharaoh is the warning that the late word does not undo the early deeds. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the history: the “you always disobeyed” is the sum of the life, and the sum is not canceled by the single word of the end, and the believer who knows this hastens the obedience and the repentance while the life remains, for the door that closes at the death is the door that the living must enter. The verse is the rebuke of the late confession, and the warning against the delay of the return.",
  "92": "“Today We will preserve your corpse so that you may become an example for those who come after you. And surely most people are heedless of Our examples!”” records the preservation of the body of Pharaoh, and the commentators note the miracle and its purpose. The verse states the decree: “Today We will preserve your corpse so that you may become an example for those who come after you,” or “fa-al-yawma nunajjeeka bi-badanika li-takoona li-man khalfaka ayah,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for God decreed that the body of Pharaoh, the tyrant who had claimed divinity, would be cast up from the sea and preserved, and the preservation of the corpse was the sign, the “ayah,” the example for the generations that would follow, and the commentators note the significance, for the body of the drowned tyrant was the proof of the drowning, the visible witness that the one who had claimed to be the lord had perished like the least of his subjects. Ibn Kathir relates the detail: the body of Pharaoh was cast up upon the shore, and the preservation of the corpse was the miracle that the verse records, and the body became the example that the following generations saw and the lesson that the heedless ignored. The verse closes with the lament: “And surely most people are heedless of Our examples!” or “wa inna katheeran mina an-nasi ‘an ayatina la-ghafiloon,” the heedlessness of the majority, the “ghaflah,” the neglect of the signs that the preservation of the corpse was meant to teach.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of God are not only the miracles of the deliverance but the monuments of the destruction, and the corpse of the tyrant is as much a sign as the parting of the sea, and the one who reflects upon the end of the tyrant is the one who learns the lesson that the tyrant himself had ignored, and the heedlessness of the majority is the warning that the signs are many and the reflectors are few. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the example: the body of Pharaoh is the “ayah” that the heedless pass by, and the believer who stops to reflect upon it is the one who extracts the lesson that the sign was meant to teach, the lesson that the tyrant perishes and the truth prevails. The verse is the record of the preservation, and the teaching of the example that the heedless neglect.",
  "93": "\"Indeed, We settled the Children of Israel in a blessed land\" moves the story past the drowning to what followed. Rescue was not the end; it was the beginning of a new responsibility. The land referred to is generally understood as the region of Syria and Palestine, described elsewhere in the Quran as blessed. The blessing lay in its fertility, its safety after years of fear, and its long history of prophets.\n\n\"And granted them good, lawful provisions\" describes the change in their circumstances. The Arabic word \"tayyibat\" covers what is pure, wholesome, and pleasant, and the phrase indicates both material abundance and lawfulness. A people who had eaten as slaves now ate as free men from what Allah had made good for them. The Quran elsewhere lists these favours in detail, including the shade of clouds, the food sent down to them in the wilderness, and water brought from rock.\n\n\"They did not differ until knowledge came to them\" makes an unexpected and important point. Their divisions did not arise from ignorance. They arose after clear knowledge had reached them. This inverts the assumption most people hold, that disagreement is caused by not knowing enough. Here, knowledge was present, and the splitting happened anyway, which means the cause lay elsewhere: envy, ambition, and unwillingness to accept a truth that limited someone's position. The Quran makes this explicit in other passages, saying that those given the Scripture differed only after knowledge came to them, out of rivalry among themselves.\n\nThis carries a warning for every religious community, including Muslims. The Prophet foretold that his community would divide as earlier communities had. Access to scripture and scholarship is no protection by itself if hearts are competitive.\n\n\"Surely your Lord will judge between them on the Day of Judgment regarding their differences\" leaves the final settlement where it belongs. Some disputes will never be resolved by argument in this world, and pretending otherwise wastes a great deal of energy. The believer's duty is to hold to what is clear, avoid causing division, and accept that the complete verdict is delivered by Allah, not by any human party.",
  "94": "\"If you ˹O Prophet˺ are in doubt about ˹these stories˺ that We have revealed to you, then ask those who read the Scripture before you\" is one of the most misread verses in this surah, so its meaning needs care. On the surface it seems to suggest the Prophet doubted his own revelation. Reliable commentators, including at-Tabari and Ibn Kathir, explain that this is a well-known style of Arabic address, where a statement is directed to one person while the real audience is everyone listening. It is like a teacher saying to the class, \"if anyone here has doubts, the evidence is available.\"\n\nThere is also a report that when the Prophet was asked about this verse, he said that he did not doubt and did not ask, and this is mentioned in the tafsir literature. His entire life supports that. A man who endured boycott, exile, and war for this message was not uncertain about its source.\n\nThe instruction to \"ask those who read the Scripture before you\" points to something historically powerful. The account of Moses, Pharaoh, the plagues, the sea, and the Children of Israel was preserved among the Jews and Christians of Arabia. An unlettered man in Mecca had no access to their books, and yet the Quran narrated these events with confidence and invited verification from the very communities who possessed the older record. That is not how a forger behaves. Surah Hud makes a similar point when it says that the Prophet did not know these stories, nor did his people, before this revelation.\n\n\"The truth has certainly come to you from your Lord, so do not be one of those who doubt\" then closes the matter with certainty rather than argument. The point of the verse is not to raise a doubt but to remove one, by showing that the evidence lies open for anyone who wishes to check.\n\nFor readers today the principle still applies. Faith in Islam is not asked for blindly. Questions have answers, and the Quran repeatedly invites people to look rather than to guess.",
  "95": "\"And do not be one of those who deny Allah's signs\" continues the address begun in the previous verse and raises the warning a level. Doubt was mentioned first, then outright rejection, because that is the order in which a heart usually falls. Uncertainty left unexamined hardens into denial, and denial eventually becomes a settled position that no evidence can shift, exactly as this surah described of the peoples who came after Noah.\n\nAgain the wording is directed at the Prophet while the real audience is everyone who hears it. This is a recognised style in the Quran, and it appears elsewhere in warnings addressed to him about associating partners with Allah or inclining toward those who do wrong. Such verses are not accusations. They show the seriousness of the matter by addressing the most protected person of all, so that no listener can imagine himself beyond the reach of the warning.\n\nThe signs being spoken of are broad. They include the verses of the Quran itself, the histories just recounted, the natural order of night and day mentioned earlier in this surah, and the evidence of Allah's power in creation. Denying them is not merely an intellectual error; it is a refusal to acknowledge what has been clearly shown.\n\n\"Or you will be one of the losers\" names the outcome with the Arabic idea of \"khusran,\" which means loss in the sense of a failed transaction, ending up with less than you started with. The Quran uses trade language for this repeatedly, because life is presented as an exchange: time, effort, and choices are spent, and something is received in return. The one who denies the signs spends his entire life and receives nothing lasting for it.\n\nPlacing this immediately after the reference to Jewish and Christian scholars who could confirm the earlier scriptures gives it added weight. Some of them recognised the truth of the message and still turned away for reasons of status and community pressure. Knowing something is true and refusing it anyway is the most complete form of loss the verse describes.",
  "96": "\"Indeed, those against whom Allah's decree ˹of torment˺ is justified will not believe\" returns to a theme the surah raised earlier and states it as a settled reality. Some people will not believe, and this is known. The wording indicates that Allah's word has become due against them, meaning the sentence has been earned and fixed by their own conduct.\n\nThis needs careful explanation, because it is easy to misread as saying that God arbitrarily condemns people and then blames them for it. The surah has already ruled that out. It stated plainly that Allah does not wrong people in the least, but it is people who wrong themselves. It also described the sealing of hearts as something that follows persistent rejection, since those communities would not believe in what they had already rejected before. The decree confirms a choice; it does not replace one. Allah's knowledge of what a person will freely choose does not force that choice, in the same way that a teacher who knows a lazy student will fail has not caused the failure.\n\nPharaoh, whose story has just been told, is the clearest illustration. He was given every opportunity over many years, and his final drowning declaration showed that even at the end his belief came only when escape was impossible.\n\nThe purpose of this statement in context is to steady the Prophet. He had been striving intensely for the guidance of his people, and other verses describe him almost destroying himself with grief over their refusal. Knowing in advance that a certain group will not respond protects a caller from measuring his worth by their reaction. His duty is to deliver clearly, not to produce belief.\n\nIt is equally important to note the limit of this. No specific living person can be identified as belonging to this group, since that knowledge belongs to Allah alone. This surah itself said that some of them will eventually believe and others will not. So nobody is written off, and the invitation continues, while the one giving it is freed from the burden of guaranteeing results.",
  "97": "\"Even if every sign were to come to them\" completes the statement begun in the previous verse about those whose rejection has hardened into a settled state. It answers the objection that people would surely believe if only they were shown enough evidence. The verse says no. For this particular kind of heart, quantity of proof is not the issue.\n\nThe wording is deliberately absolute. Not some signs, not many signs, but every sign, meaning nothing at all could be added that would change the result. Pharaoh's story, told in detail just before, demonstrates it exactly. He saw the staff, the hand, the collapse of his magicians, then a whole series of afflictions described elsewhere in the Quran, each one followed by a promise to submit and each promise broken. Anyone claiming that another miracle would have convinced him has not read his history.\n\nThe Meccans made the same demand. They asked for a spring to gush from the ground, for a house of gold, for the Prophet to ascend into the sky and bring back a book they could read. Surah al-An'am answers such requests directly, saying that even if angels had been sent down to them and the dead had spoken to them, they still would not have believed unless Allah willed it. Surah al-Hijr adds an even stronger image, describing people who would keep climbing through an opened gate in the sky and still insist their eyes had been deceived.\n\n\"Until they see the painful punishment\" identifies the one thing that will finally end their denial, and by then it is not belief in any meaningful sense. It is simply the end of the argument, the same worthless recognition Pharaoh reached in the water.\n\nThere is a correction here for a common assumption. People often imagine that faith is a purely intellectual matter, so that the right proof delivered to the right person must produce belief. The Quran presents faith as involving the whole person, including his willingness, his humility, and his readiness to accept what the truth will cost him. Evidence reaches the mind, but a mind can be locked from the inside.",
  "98": "“If only there had been a society which believed ˹before seeing the torment˺ and, therefore, benefited from its belief, like the people of Jonah. When they believed, We lifted from them the torment of disgrace in this world and allowed them enjoyment for a while.”” records the single exception among the destroyed nations, and the commentators note the uniqueness of the people of Jonah. The verse opens with the regret: “If only there had been a society which believed before seeing the torment and, therefore, benefited from its belief, like the people of Jonah,” or “fa-law la kanat qaryatun amanat fa-nafa‘aha imanuha illa qawma Yoonusa,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the verse declares that the people of Jonah were the single community in the history of the destroyed nations that believed in time and benefited from its belief, and the uniqueness of their case is the point of the verse, for every other nation that rejected its messenger was destroyed, while the people of Jonah believed and were spared. Ibn Kathir explains the story: Jonah, or Yunus, was sent to the people of Nineveh, and he called them and they rejected him, and he left them in anger, and when the signs of the punishment appeared, the people believed and repented, and the verse records that their belief benefited them, for the punishment that had approached was lifted. The verse then states the outcome: “When they believed, We lifted from them the torment of disgrace in this world and allowed them enjoyment for a while,” or “lamma amanoo kashafna ‘anhum ‘athaba al-khizyi fi al-hayati ad-dunya wa matta‘nahum ila heen,” the lifting of the “athab al-khizyi,” the torment of the disgrace, and the grant of the enjoyment for a time, and the commentators note the contrast with the other nations, for the people of Jonah believed and were spared, while the others rejected and were destroyed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repentance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the door of the return is never closed until the decree has fallen, and the people of Jonah are the proof that the belief in the final hour is still accepted, and the one who turns to the Lord with the sincerity of the people of Nineveh is the one whose repentance is received and whose punishment is lifted, for the mercy of the Lord is the mercy that the sincere return invites. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the great exception that proves the rule, and the believer reads in it the comfort of the return: the people who believed before the torment benefited from their belief, and the one who believes before the decree has believed in time, and the lifting of the punishment is the reward of the return that the verse records. The verse is the record of the exception, and the teaching of the repentance that the mercy receives.",
  "99": "\"Had your Lord so willed ˹O Prophet˺, all ˹people˺ on earth would have certainly believed, every single one of them!\" addresses the grief of a messenger whose people would not respond. It begins by establishing what Allah could have done. Universal belief was entirely within His power, and the emphatic wording leaves no room for exceptions.\n\nSince He could have done this and did not, the arrangement is deliberate. Human beings were created with the capacity to choose, and a world in which everyone believed by compulsion would not be a place of testing at all. It would be like a teacher who fills in the answers for the students and then awards them grades. The Quran states elsewhere that Allah created death and life to test which of us is best in deeds, and testing requires the real possibility of failing.\n\n\"Would you then force people to become believers?\" turns the matter into a question the Prophet must answer himself. If Allah, with complete power over hearts, chose not to compel, how could a human being take that role upon himself? The question is gentle but decisive. It sets a limit on the messenger's responsibility that he cannot cross.\n\nThis verse is one of the foundations of the Islamic position that belief cannot be forced, alongside the well-known statement in Surah al-Baqarah that there is no compulsion in religion. The reason is not merely moral but practical: faith is an act of the heart, and hearts cannot be coerced. A person can be made to say words and perform motions, and none of it produces belief. That is why the Quran describes hypocrisy as a disease rather than a solution.\n\nIt is worth being clear about what the verse does not say. It does not say all beliefs are equally true, since the surah has spent many verses arguing that some claims are false and invented. It says that the response must be freely given.\n\nFor anyone who carries the message today, in a family or a community, the instruction is the same: present it clearly, live it honestly, and stop at the point where pressure begins.",
  "100": "\"It is not for any soul to believe except by Allah's leave\" continues the answer to the Prophet's longing for his people's guidance. Belief is not something a human being manufactures in himself or produces in another. It comes with permission from Allah, which is why no amount of pressure, argument, or emotional appeal can force it into a heart.\n\nThe Arabic word \"idhn\" means permission or leave. Saying that belief happens by Allah's leave does not mean people are helpless puppets, and the surah has been careful about this throughout. It has already stated that Allah does not wrong anyone and that people wrong themselves, and it described sealing as the consequence of persistent refusal. The two truths are held together: Allah's will governs everything that happens, and human choice is real and accountable. A person who sincerely seeks guidance is given it, as the Quran says of those who strive in His cause being guided to His paths. A person who rejects it repeatedly is left to what he has chosen.\n\nUnderstanding this changes how a believer relates to his own faith. If belief is permitted by Allah rather than achieved by cleverness, then having it is a gift to be grateful for rather than an accomplishment to feel superior about. It also explains why the Prophet frequently asked Allah to keep his heart firm, in a supplication recorded in the collections of hadith addressing the One who turns hearts and asking Him to fix his heart upon His religion.\n\n\"And He will bring His wrath upon those who are unmindful\" names the other side. The Arabic word \"rijs\" here indicates filth, defilement, or a state of punishment, and commentators have understood it as the disgrace and torment that settle on those who refuse to think.\n\nThe description \"unmindful\" is important. The Arabic points to those who do not use their reason, not those who lack intelligence. These were people capable of running trade caravans across deserts and composing intricate poetry. Their failure was not in ability but in refusing to apply that ability to the one question that mattered most.",
  "101": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Consider all that is in the heavens and the earth!\"\" issues an open invitation to look, and it is remarkable how confident it is. There is no fear that careful observation will damage faith. The Quran repeatedly sends people out to examine the world, and it treats the universe as evidence rather than as a distraction from evidence.\n\nThe Arabic verb here means to look with attention and reflection, not to glance. It is the difference between passing a tree every day and actually studying how it grows. The instruction covers everything above and everything below: the order of the stars, the movement of the sun and moon, rain, mountains, seas, plants, animals, and the human body itself. Elsewhere the Quran asks people to look at the camel and how it is created, at the sky and how it is raised, at the mountains and how they are fixed, at the earth and how it is spread.\n\nThis attitude helped shape the intellectual life of early Muslim civilisation, where the study of astronomy, medicine, and mathematics was pursued by scholars who saw no conflict between examining creation and honouring the Creator.\n\n\"Yet neither signs nor warners are of any benefit to those who refuse to believe\" adds the sober qualification. Two kinds of evidence are named. Signs are what the universe displays, available to anyone who looks. Warners are the messengers who explain and call. Both are provided, and neither works on a person determined not to be persuaded.\n\nTwo people can stand under the same night sky. One sees design, precision, and a Maker worth worshipping. The other sees only material. The difference is not in the sky.\n\nThis connects to the surah's earlier statement that Allah does not wrong anyone. The evidence was placed in plain view, and the messengers were sent. Nothing was withheld. When someone still refuses, the failure is located exactly where the surah has been placing it all along, in the human being who would not look honestly at what was in front of him.",
  "102": "\"Are they waiting for ˹anything˺ except the same torments that befell those before them?\" turns the tables on the deniers who kept demanding that the promised punishment arrive. They thought they were waiting for nothing. The question points out that they are in fact waiting for something very specific, whether they realise it or not, and that history has already shown what it looks like.\n\nThe Arabic word for these days indicates the notable days of a people, the events that mark and define them. The Quran has just recounted several: the flood in Noah's time, the destruction of communities after him, and the drowning of Pharaoh's army. The Meccans travelled past the ruins of destroyed peoples on their trade routes and knew these stories well. The pattern was not hidden from them.\n\n\"Say, \"Keep waiting then! I too am waiting with you.\"\" is an extraordinary response, and its calmness is its strength. The Prophet is not told to argue further or to plead. He is told to join them in waiting, because he is entirely confident about what the waiting will produce. Two parties are anticipating the same outcome with completely opposite expectations, and only one of them can be right.\n\nThis is a form of challenge that a person can only make when he is certain. It resembles Noah's challenge earlier in this surah, when he invited his people to gather their plans and their gods and act against him without delay, and it resembles Moses telling the magicians to cast whatever they wished. In each case a lone man faced a hostile majority and left the outcome to Allah.\n\nWithin a few years the waiting ended. The leaders who mocked in Mecca were defeated at Badr, and the city that expelled the Prophet received him peacefully.\n\nThere is a wider principle here worth noticing. The Quran often responds to mockery not with heated defence but with quiet confidence, and that response tends to unsettle opponents far more than argument does. A person who is certain does not need to raise his voice.",
  "103": "\"Then We saved Our messengers and those who believed\" gives the other half of the historical pattern just mentioned. The days that befell earlier peoples were not only days of destruction; they were also days of rescue. Every account in this surah has had two endings. Noah and those with him were carried in the Ark while the deniers drowned. Moses and the Children of Israel crossed the sea while Pharaoh's army did not. Jonah's people were spared when they turned back in time.\n\nThe phrase \"and those who believed\" is worth attention. Salvation was not restricted to the prophets themselves. Ordinary believers, often few in number and without any status, were included. The young men who believed in Moses while fearing Pharaoh and their own chiefs were carried out with him.\n\n\"For it is Our duty to save the believers\" is the striking part of the verse. The wording states this rescue as an obligation, using a word that indicates something binding and rightfully due. Nobody imposes duties on Allah from outside; He has bound Himself by His own promise, which is what makes it certain. Surah al-An'am contains a similar statement, where Allah says He has taken it upon Himself to be merciful. That self-imposed commitment is the strongest guarantee that exists, since it comes from the One who never breaks His word, as this surah affirmed when it said that Allah's promise is always true.\n\nFor the Muslims listening in Mecca, poor and harassed and seeing no sign of relief, this was not an abstract theological point. It was a promise that their situation had an end, even if they could not see it yet.\n\nIt is worth adding a caution the Quran itself supplies. Rescue does not always mean escaping every hardship in this world. Believers have been killed for their faith, and the Quran honours them rather than treating them as abandoned. The salvation promised is real and certain, but its timing and its form are decided by Allah, as this surah has repeatedly said about the appointed term.",
  "104": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O humanity! If you are in doubt of my faith\"\" begins the closing section of the surah, and the address widens again beyond the Meccans to all people. What follows is a public declaration of exactly what the Prophet stood for, phrased so that nobody could remain confused about it.\n\n\"Then ˹know that˺ I do not worship those ˹idols˺ you worship instead of Allah\" states the negative side first, which is the correct order in Islamic belief. The declaration of faith itself begins by denying every false object of worship before affirming the one true God. Clearing the ground comes before building. A field full of weeds is not improved by planting good seed among them.\n\n\"But I worship Allah, Who has the power to cause your death\" then gives the positive affirmation, and the description chosen is pointed. Of all the attributes that could have been mentioned, the verse selects the One who takes souls. The Meccan idols could not give life, provide, protect, or answer prayer, and above all they had no authority over death. Everyone standing there, including those threatening the Prophet, was subject to a power none of them controlled. Reminding them of this cut through all argument, because death was the one certainty they could not dispute.\n\nThere may also be a quiet answer to their threats in this. They had warned him of what they would do to him, and he replies that the One he worships is the One who decides when any person dies. That belief is precisely what made him unafraid of them, as it had made Noah unafraid earlier in the surah.\n\n\"And I have been commanded, 'Be one of the believers,'\" closes the verse by locating the source of his position. He is not presenting a personal philosophy or a movement he founded. He was commanded, and he is following. The next several verses continue this list of commands, and reading them together shows the Prophet describing his religion as a set of instructions received, not opinions chosen.",
  "105": "\"And, 'Be steadfast in faith in all uprightness'\" continues the list of commands the Prophet was given, and it names the shape of the religion he was told to keep. The Arabic word behind \"in all uprightness\" is \"hanif,\" which describes turning away from every false path and inclining wholly toward the one true God. It was already associated with Ibrahim, who is repeatedly described in the Quran as neither a Jew nor a Christian but upright and submitting to Allah.\n\nUsing this word carried real force in Mecca. The Quraysh honoured Ibrahim as their ancestor and took pride in maintaining his sanctuary, yet they had filled it with idols. Telling the Prophet to be upright in this precise sense reminded everyone that the religion he brought was not new. It was the restoration of what their own forefather actually practised, and it was they who had departed from it.\n\nThe command to set one's face steadily toward the religion also implies constancy. A face turned in one direction does not keep swinging around to look elsewhere. This matches what the surah has already said about the Prophet's instructions: follow what is revealed, be patient, and do not be moved by the reactions of others.\n\n\"And do not be one of the polytheists\" adds the boundary. The Arabic term \"shirk\" means associating partners with Allah, giving to something created what belongs only to the Creator, whether in worship, ultimate trust, or the authority to make things lawful and forbidden. The surah has been dismantling this idea throughout, asking whether any of their associate-gods can originate creation and resurrect it, or guide to the truth, and answering each time that only Allah does.\n\nThe instruction is phrased as not being among such people, which reaches further than avoiding the act itself. Belief is shaped by company, and a person who lives entirely inside a community devoted to something false will find its assumptions seeping into him without any conscious decision. Staying upright therefore involves both what one worships and what one allows to surround and influence the heart.",
  "106": "\"And 'Do not invoke, instead of Allah, what can neither benefit nor harm you'\" moves from the general prohibition of associating partners with Allah to the specific act at its centre: calling upon something other than Him. The Arabic word \"du'a\" here means to call, invoke, or supplicate, and it lies at the heart of worship. In a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi and others, the Prophet said that supplication is worship, because a person who calls on someone in need has admitted that this someone can hear, can respond, and has power over the outcome.\n\nThe test the verse applies is entirely practical. Can this thing benefit you? Can it harm you? If the answer is no, then calling on it is not merely forbidden but pointless. Ibrahim used exactly this reasoning with his father and his people, asking why they worshipped what could not hear, see, or benefit them in any way, and telling them that these objects could neither help them nor help themselves.\n\nThe Meccans would have said their idols brought rain, protection, and success in trade, and that they were only intermediaries who would bring them closer to Allah. The Quran records that claim and rejects it. No creature has independent power to benefit or harm, so no creature can be a route to God through invocation. Notably, earlier in this surah the Prophet himself was told to announce that he had no power to benefit or protect himself except by the will of Allah. If that is said about the Messenger, it settles the matter about anything else.\n\n\"For if you do, then you will certainly be one of the wrongdoers\" states the consequence in strong terms. The word \"zulm,\" wrongdoing, means putting something out of its proper place, and directing worship away from the Creator is the most serious misplacement possible. Surah Luqman describes associating partners with Allah as a great wrong.\n\nBecause this warning is addressed to the Prophet, its seriousness is unmistakable. If such an act would be catastrophic for him, nobody else can imagine himself excused from it.",
  "107": "\"And 'If Allah touches you with harm, none can undo it except Him'\" completes the list of commands with the belief that makes all the previous ones possible. Once a person is certain that harm and good come from Allah alone, calling on anything else becomes not only wrong but absurd.\n\nThe verb \"touches\" is gentle, and it is chosen deliberately. Even the harm that reaches a person is a touch, allowed for a purpose and measured exactly. Illness, poverty, fear, and loss all arrive within limits set by the One who sent them. And since He sent it, only He can lift it. Every doctor, every helper, every remedy is a means He created, and none of them works unless He permits.\n\n\"And if He intends good for you, none can withhold His bounty\" gives the other half, and it is equally freeing. No enemy, no rival, no unjust authority can block what Allah has decided to give. Provision that has been written cannot be stolen by someone else's plotting. The Prophet expressed this in his advice to Ibn Abbas, recorded by at-Tirmidhi, telling him that if the whole community gathered to benefit him they could only benefit him with what Allah had already written for him, and if they gathered to harm him they could only harm him with what Allah had already written against him.\n\nTogether these two statements dissolve most of what people fear. Fear usually rests on the belief that someone else holds our fate. This verse relocates that power entirely.\n\n\"He grants it to whoever He wills of His servants\" preserves His freedom in giving. Nobody can demand or calculate their way into His favour, and differences in provision are part of the test rather than a measure of worth.\n\n\"And He is the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" ends the sequence of commands on mercy rather than on threat. This matters after so many verses about punishment. The One who controls harm and good is also the One who forgives repeatedly and treats His servants with compassion, which is why turning to Him is never a hopeless act.",
  "108": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O humanity! The truth has surely come to you from your Lord.\"\" delivers the summary of the entire surah in a single announcement. Everything that came before has been leading here: the arguments about the Quran's origin, the histories of Noah and Moses, the exposure of guesswork and invented religion. The conclusion is that the truth has arrived, and it arrived from the Lord of the people being addressed, not from a stranger and not from human imagination.\n\n\"So whoever chooses to be guided, it is only for their own good\" sets out the first consequence. Guidance benefits the one who accepts it and nobody else. Allah gains nothing from human obedience, since He is described in this very surah as the Self-Sufficient who owns whatever is in the heavens and the earth. A person who follows the truth is doing himself a favour, not doing God one.\n\n\"And whoever chooses to stray, it is only to their own loss\" states the reverse with equal clarity. This echoes what the surah said earlier, that Allah does not wrong people in the least but people wrong themselves. There is a strong parallel in a sacred hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim, where Allah tells His servants that they can neither reach His harm to harm Him nor reach His benefit to benefit Him, and that He is only recording their deeds for them and repaying them accordingly.\n\n\"And I am not a keeper over you\" closes with a precise definition of the Prophet's role. The Arabic word \"wakil\" indicates one who is put in charge of another's affairs, responsible for managing and guaranteeing them. He was sent to convey, warn, and give good news, not to control hearts or force outcomes. This restates in the plainest terms what the surah asked a few verses earlier, whether he would force people to become believers.\n\nThe verse leaves each listener holding his own decision. Nobody else can carry it, and nobody else will answer for it.",
  "109": "\"And follow what is revealed to you\" is the closing instruction of the surah, and it is addressed to the Prophet after a long chapter of opposition, mockery, and demands for compromise. The Meccans had offered wealth and leadership if he would soften the message, and they had asked for a different Quran or for parts of it to be changed. The answer here is simple: follow what is revealed, not what is negotiated.\n\nThe word \"follow\" places him under the revelation rather than above it. He did not select its content, adjust its rulings to suit circumstances, or hold back the parts that were hardest to deliver. The Quran even records verses that corrected him, and he conveyed those too, which is itself an argument for the truthfulness of the message he carried.\n\n\"And be patient until Allah passes His judgment\" gives the second instruction, and patience here is not a vague sentiment. The Arabic word \"sabr\" means holding oneself firm, restraining the self from collapse, complaint, or abandoning the path. It is the strength to continue doing right while the situation stays difficult and while the promised relief has not yet arrived. The surah has spoken repeatedly of an appointed term that cannot be advanced or delayed, and patience is what fills the gap between the promise and its arrival.\n\nThe pairing of these two commands is the whole prophetic method in miniature. Do the work as instructed, and endure what follows. Nothing is said about producing results, because the previous verse has just removed that responsibility from him.\n\n\"For He is the Best of Judges\" ends the chapter with the reason patience is reasonable rather than merely noble. Human judges are limited: they can be deceived, they lack full knowledge, and they cannot always enforce what they decide. Allah judges with complete knowledge of every intention and complete power to carry out the verdict. A person who genuinely believes his case will be heard by such a judge can afford to wait, and can afford to leave the correcting of injustice to the One who sees all of it."
};
